Checking the case of  $p(x) = ax + b$ , we find that this works if and only if  $a = \pm 1$ .

Now, we consider the case of  $\deg p \ge 2$ . Since differentiation decreases the multiplicity of existing complex roots by  $1$ , a necessary condition for the conclusion to hold is
\begin{align*}
0 &\equiv \Big(p\big(p(x)\big) - x\Big)' & \pmod{p(x) - x}
&\equiv p'(x)p'\big(p(x)\big) - 1 &\pmod{p(x) - x}
&\equiv \big(p'(x) - 1\big)\big(p'(x) + 1\big) &\pmod{p(x) - x}
\end{align*}
If  $p(x) - x$  has a root of multiplicity  $n > 1$  at some  $r\in\mathbb{C}$ , then  $\big(p(x) - x\big)' = p'(x) - 1$  must have the same root with multiplicity  $n - 1$ , so one factor of  $x - r$  must divide  $p'(x) + 1$ . However, this is impossible because  $p'(x) + 1$  is relatively prime to  $p'(x) - 1$ . On the other hand, if  $p(x) - x$  has no roots of multiplicity greater than  $1$ , then it shares no roots with  $p'(x) - 1$ , and hence must divide  $p'(x) + 1$ . This is impossible for degree reasons, and we have exhausted all the cases to show that  $\deg p \ge 2$  is impossible.

The answer is  $p(x) = x+c$  or  $p(x)=-x+c$  for some constant  $c$ . We will show that all  $p$  with  $\deg p \geq 2$  fail and leave the case  $\deg p \leq 1$  as an exercise to the reader. 

We note that by Taylor expansion, we have
\begin{align*}
p(p(x)) &= p\Big( x + (p(x)-x)\Big) 
&= p(x) + p'(x)\, (p(x)-x) + p''(x)\frac{(p(x)-x)^2}{2!} + \dots 
&\equiv p(x) + p'(x)\, (p(x)-x) & \pmod{(p(x)-x)^2} 
p(p(x)) - x &\equiv (p(x)-x)(p'(x)+1) & \pmod{(p(x)-x)^2}.
\end{align*}
Thus, we deduce that  $p(x)-x$  divides  $p'(x)+1$ , which contradicts by degree.

Lindstrom-Gessel-Viennot lemma! Thanks to Yannick Yao for hinting me how to weigh the path.

First, we turn the generating function back to recurrence relation.

<span style="color:red">**Claim.**</span> We have  $c_0=0$ ,  $c_1=1$ , and  $$ c_n = 2(c_1c_{n-1} + c_2c_{n-2} + \dots + c_{n-1}c_1) + 3c_{n-1}. $$  *Proof.* We can see that  $c_0=0$  from directly plugging in  $x=0$  in the expression. Next, note that if  $C(x) = \sum_{k=0}^\infty c_kx^k$ , then  $$ 2\,C(x)^2 - (1-3x)C(x) + x = 0. $$  Comparing the  $x^n$  coefficient on both sides gives the claim.  $\blacksquare$ -----

Next, consider the following grid, where horizontal edges are weighted alternating between  $5$  and  $2$  in the following fashion:
[asy]
int n=8;
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(9pt));
for(int i=0; i<=n; ++i){
    draw((i,0)--(i,i), gray);
    for(int j=i; j<n; ++j){
    	if((j-i)%2 == 0){
    		draw((j,i)--(j+1,i), red);
            label(" $5$ ", (j+0.5,i), S, red);
        }
        else{
        	draw((j,i)--(j+1,i), blue);
            label(" $2$ ", (j+0.5,i), S, blue);
        }
    }
}
draw((-0.5,-0.5)--(n+0.5,n+0.5), deepgreen);
dot(" $(0,0)$ ", (0,0), NW);
[/asy]
Consider all paths from  $(0,0)$  to  $(n-1,n-1)$  without going above the green diagonal.
The weight of the path is the product of weights of all edges used in the path.

<span style="color:red">**Claim.**</span> The sum of weights across all paths from  $(0,0)$  to  $(n-1,n-1)$  is  $c_n$ .

*Proof.* Let  $d_n$  denote the sum of weights across all paths from  $(0,0)$  to  $(n-1,n-1)$ , but with weighting of edges swapped from  $5$  to  $2$  and  $2$  to  $5$ . We have the vacuous case  $c_0=d_0=1$  because weight of empty path is  $1$ . Then, by caseworking on the first point that the path hits the diagonal, we can obtain the recursion
\begin{align*}
c_n &= 5(c_1d_{n-1} + c_2d_{n-2} + \dots + c_{n-1}d_1) 
d_n &= 2(c_1d_{n-1} + c_2d_{n-2} + \dots + c_{n-1}d_1)
\end{align*}
for all  $n\geq 2$ . Thus,  $c_n/5 = d_n/2$  for all  $n\geq 2$ . Plugging this into the first equation yields
\begin{align*}
c_n &= 2(c_1c_{n-1} + c_2c_{n-2} + \dots + c_{n-2}c_2) + 5c_{n-1} 
&= 2(c_1c_{n-1} + c_2c_{n-2} + \dots + c_{n-2}c_2 + c_{n-1}c_1) + 3c_{n-1},
\end{align*}
as desired.  $\blacksquare$ -----
Now, we have the setup to use Lindstrom-Gessel-Viennot lemma. Place points
\begin{align*}
A_1 = (0,0), \quad A_2 = (1,1), \quad &\dots, \quad A_n = (n-1,n-1) 
B_1 = (0,0), \quad B_2 = (-1,-1), \quad &\dots, \quad B_n = (-(n-1), -(n-1)),
\end{align*}
so that the number of paths from  $A_i$  to  $B_j$  is  $c_{i+j-1}$ . There is only one way to produce non-crossing path: from  $A_1$  to  $B_1$ , ...,  $A_n$  to  $B_n$  in the obvious way, which gets weight  $10^{\binom n2}$ , as shown in the following diagram.
[asy]
int n=4;
size(7cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(9pt));
for(int i=-n; i<=n; ++i){
    draw((i,-n)--(i,i), gray);
    for(int j=i; j<n; ++j){
    	if((j-i)%2 == 0){
    		draw((j,i)--(j+1,i), red);
            label(" $5$ ", (j+0.5,i), S, red);
        }
        else{
        	draw((j,i)--(j+1,i), blue);
            label(" $2$ ", (j+0.5,i), S, blue);
        }
    }
}
dot(" $A_1=B_1$ ", (0,0), NW);
for(int i=1; i<=n; ++i){
	dot(" $A_" + ((string)(i+1)) + "$ ", (i,i), NW);
    dot(" $B_" + ((string)(i+1)) + "$ ", (-i,-i), NW);
    draw((i,i)--(i,-i)--(-i,-i), black+linewidth(1.5));
}
draw((-n-0.5,-n-0.5)--(n+0.5,n+0.5), deepgreen);
[/asy]
Hence, by Lindstrom-Gessel Viennot lemma, we get that  $\det M = 10^{\binom n2}$ .

