Note that  $\tan x$  intersects  $x$  at least once on every interval  $(-\pi/2+n\pi,\pi/2+n\pi)$  for integers  $n$  by IVT. There can also only be one intersection in each interval, since if  $x=a,b$  work in the same interval, then  $b-a<\int_a^b\sec^2 x=\tan b- \tan a=b-a$ . It's not hard to see then that  $r_{n+1}-r_n>\pi$  since  $\tan(r_n+\pi)=r_n<r_{n+1}$  and  $\tan x$  is increasing over each interval. Note also that  $r_{n+1}-r_n<3\pi/2$  since  $r_n\ge n\pi$ .

Now, since  $0<r_{n+1}-r_n-\pi<\pi/2$ , we have  $r_{n+1}-r_n-\pi< \tan(r_{n+1}-r_n-\pi)=\frac{r_{n+1}-r_n}{1+r_nr_{n+1}}\le \frac{r_{n+1}-r_n}{1+\pi n(\pi(n+1))}$  by tangent subtraction. But just let  $x=r_{n+1}-r_n-\pi$  ... and solve the inequality in  $x$ . You get precisely the desired bound.

The derivative of  $\tan x - x$  is  $\sec^2 x - 1 \geq 0$ , so the function  $\tan x - x$  is increasing in each of the intervals  $\left[0,\tfrac\pi 2\right)$ ,  $\left[\pi, \tfrac{3\pi}2\right)$ ,  $\left[2\pi, \tfrac{5\pi}2\right)$ ,  $\dots$ . Thus, except  $\left[0,\tfrac\pi 2\right)$ , there is exactly one solution in each of the intervals. Hence,  $r_n \in \left(n\pi, n\pi + \tfrac\pi 2\right)$ .

Let  $s_n = r_n - n\pi \in \left(0,\tfrac{\pi}2\right)$ . Then,  $\tan s_1 < \tan s_2 < \dots$ , implying that  $s_1 < s_2 < \dots$ , which proves the first inequality  $s_{n+1}-s_n > 0$ , or equivalently  $r_{n+1}-r_n\pi > 0$ . To prove the second inequality, note that
\begin{align*}
s_{n+1} - s_n < \tan(s_{n+1}-s_n) &= \frac{\tan s_{n+1} - \tan s_n}{1- \tan s_{n+1} \tan s_n} 
s_{n+1} - s_n &< \frac{s_{n+1} - s_n + \pi}{1+ r_{n+1}r_n} < \frac{s_{n+1}-s_n+\pi}{1 + (n^2+n)\pi^2} 
(1 + (n^2+n)\pi^2)(s_{n+1}-s_n) &< s_{n+1} - s_n + \pi 
(n^2+n)\pi^2(s_{n+1}-s_n) &< \pi 
s_{n+1}-s_n &< \frac{1}{(n^2+n)\pi},
\end{align*}
as desired.

