Let  $0\leq x\leq1$ , and fix  $k$  and  $m$ . The expression  $(1+y)^{n-1}(1+xy)^k$  expands to a polynomial in  $x$  and  $y$  by the binomial theorem. The coefficient of  $y^{n-1}$  is equal to  $\sum_{a=0}^k\binom{n-1}a\binom kax^a$ . This expression is also equal to
\begin{align*}
(1+y)^{n-1}(x(1+y)+(1-x))^k&=(1+y)^{n-1}\sum_{c=0}^kx^c(1-x)^{k-c}(1+y)^c
&=\sum_{c=0}^kx^c(1-x)^{k-c}(1+y)^{n-1+c}
\end{align*}
so the  $y^{n-1}$  coefficient is equal to  $$ \sum_{c=0}^kx^c(1-x)^{k-c}\binom{n-1+c}c. $$   Since  $\binom{n-1+c}c=\frac{(n-1+c)\cdots(n+1)n}{c!}$ , this is a polynomial in  $n$  with all nonnegative coefficients. Since  $x^c(1-x)^{k-c}\geq0$ , this means the  $y^{n-1}$  is a polynomial in  $n$  with all nonnegative coefficients. Therefore,  $\sum_{a=0}^k\binom{n-1}a\binom kax^a$  is a polynomial in  $n$  with all nonnegative coefficients, so  $\sum_{a=0}^k\binom{n-1}a\binom kax^{m+a}$  is also a polynomial in  $n$  with all nonnegative coefficients. Taking the integral from  $x=0$  to  $1$  implies  $$ \int_0^1\sum_{a=0}^k\binom{n-1}a\binom kax^{m+a}\,dx=\sum_{a=0}^k\binom{n-1}a\binom ka\frac1{m+a+1} $$   is also a polynomial in  $n$  with nonnegative coefficients. Multiplying by  $\frac{(n+m)!}{(n-1)!m!}$ , which also has nonnegative coefficients gives a polynomial in  $n$  with nonnegative coefficients. This is equal to
\begin{align*}
\sum_{a=0}^k\binom{n-1}a\binom ka\frac1{m+a+1}\frac{(n+m)!}{(n-1)!m!}&=\sum_{a=0}^k\binom ka\frac{(n-1)!}{a!(n-a-1)!}\frac1{m+a+1}\frac{(n+m)!}{(n-1)!m!}
&=\sum_{a=0}^k\binom ka\frac1{a!(n-a-1)!}\frac{(m+a)!}{(m+a+1)!}\frac{(n+m)!}{m!}
&=\sum_{a=0}^k\binom ka\binom{n+m}{m+a+1}\binom{m+a}m
&=\sum_{a=0}^k\binom ka\binom{m+a}m\sum_{z=1}^n\binom{z+m-1}{m+a}
&=\sum_{z=1}^n\sum_{a=0}^k\binom ka\binom{m+a}m\binom{z+m-1}{m+a}
&=\sum_{z=1}^n\sum_{a=0}^k\binom ka\frac{(m+a)!}{m!a!}\frac{(z+m-1)!}{(m+a)!(z-a-1)!}
&=\sum_{z=1}^n\sum_{a=0}^k\binom ka\frac{(z-1)!}{(z-a-1)!a!}\frac{(z+m-1)!}{(z-1)!m!}
&=\sum_{z=1}^n\sum_{a=0}^k\binom ka\binom{z-1}a\binom{z+m-1}m
&=\sum_{z=1}^n\binom{z+m-1}m\binom{z+k-1}k,
\end{align*}
which is the desired expression by stars and bars.

