Answer is all prime powers.

Let  $a_i = \{a r^i\}$  be the geometric progression where  $ 0 \le i \le k-2$ . Then  $\frac{d_{j+1} - d_j}{d_{k+1-j} - d_{k-j}}=\frac{d_{j+1} - d_j}{n/d_j - n/d_{j+1}} = \frac{n}{d_j d_{j+1}}$  is a power of  $r$ . From this,  $\frac{\frac{n}{d_j d_{j+1}}}{\frac{n}{d_{j+1} d_{j+2} }} = \frac{d_{j+2}}{d_j}$  is a power of  $r$ . Thus, if  $d_2 = p$  where  $p$  is a prime number,  $$ d_m = 
\begin{cases}
  r^{\frac{m-1}{2}} & \text{if } m \text{ is odd} 
  p \cdot r^\frac{m-2}{2} & \text{if } m \text{ is even}
\end{cases} $$  Note that  $r$  is a prime number,  $p$  is the smallest prime divisor of  $n$  and  $d_2-1 = p-1 = a r^m$  for some  $m$ . Thus  $m = 0$  and  $a = p-1$ . If  $p\neq r$  then  $d_3-d_2 = r-p = r^j (p-1)$  gives  $j=0$  and  $r-p = p-1$ . This is a contradiction because  $d_3-d_2 = d_2-d_1$ . Thus  $r=p$  and it is obvious that all prime powers work.

