To specify the problem, we manually define  $U$  to the the point on  $AB$  such that  $\angle ACU = 90^\circ$ , and we let  $F$  be the point on  $BC$  such that  $AF=FC$ . AlphaGeometry suggests we construct the midpoints of  $AU$  and  $DU$ ; call them  $K$  and  $L$ , respectively.

Note that since  $\angle ACU=90^\circ$ ,  $K$  lines on the perpendicular bisector of  $AC$ , so  $K$ ,  $F$ , and  $E$  are collinear. 

First, we'll show that  $KLCED$  is cyclic. To this end, we have   $$ \angle LDE = 180^\circ - \angle LDB = \angle 180^\circ - \angle UDB = 180^\circ - \angle UCB = 180^\circ - (90^\circ - \angle C) = 90^\circ + \angle C, $$   and we have   $$ \angle LKE = 180^\circ - \angle AFE= 180^\circ - (90^\circ - \angle FAC) = 90^\circ + \angle C. $$   This gives  $K$ ,  $L$ ,  $D$ ,  $E$  lie on a circle.

Next,   $$ \angle EKC = \angle EKA = 90^\circ -\angle A $$   and   $$ \angle EDC = 180^\circ - \angle BDC = 180^\circ - (90^\circ + \angle A) = 90^\circ -\angle A, $$   so  $C$  lies on this circle as well.

Finally, we observe that  $\triangle CLU \sim \triangle CEB$ , since  $\angle CUL = \angle CUD = \angle CBD$  and  $\angle BEC = \angle DEC = 180^\circ -\angle DLC = \angle ULC$ .

In particular, this gives  $\triangle MEB \sim \triangle CDU$ , since  $\angle CUL = \angle CBE$  and   $$ \frac{MB}{EB} = \frac{CB}{2 \cdot EB} = \frac{CU}{2\cdot LU} = \frac{CU}{DU}. $$   This gives  $\angle MEB = \angle CDU = \angle CBU$ , and we are done. 

