Observe that the first angle condition implies  $\overline{AD}$  intersects  $(ABC)$  again at the point  $P$  such that  $\overline{BP} \parallel \overline{AC}$ , and the second angle condition implies (by the Mathcounts angle chasing fact) that if  $\overline{BD}$  and  $\overline{CD}$  intersect  $(ABC)$  again at  $X$  and  $X'$  then  $\overline{XX'}$  is diameter.

We use complex numbers with  $(ABC)$  as the unit circle, letting  $A=a$  etc. so  $P=\tfrac{ac}{b}$ ,  $X=x$ , and  $X'=-x$ . Since we should have  $\overline{BX} \cap \overline{AP}=\overline{CX'} \cap \overline{AP}=D$ , we have
\begin{align*}
\frac{bx(a+\tfrac{ac}{b})-\tfrac{a^2c}{b}(b+x)}{bx-\tfrac{a^2c}{b}}&=\frac{-cx(a+\tfrac{ac}{b})-\tfrac{a^2c}{b}(c-x)}{-cx-\tfrac{a^2c}{b}}
\frac{ab^2x+abcx-a^2bc-a^2cx}{b^2x-a^2c}&=\frac{abcx+ac^2x+a^2c^2-a^2cx}{bcx+a^2c}
((b^2+bc-ac)x-abc)(bcx+a^2c)&=((bc+c^2-ac)x+ac^2)(b^2x-a^2c)
bc(b^2+bc-ac)x-ab^2c^2x+a^2c(b^2+bc-ac)x-a^3bc^2&=b^2(bc+c^2-ac)x+ab^2c^2x-a^2c(bc+c^2-ac)x-a^3c^3
(ab^2c-abc^2)x^2+(a^2c(b^2+c^2+2bc-2ac)-2ab^2c^2)x+a^3c^3-a^3bc^2&=0
b(b-c)x^2+(a(b^2+c^2+2bc-2ac)-2b^2c)x+a^2c(c-b)&=0.
\end{align*}
On the other hand, by intersecting  $\overline{BX}$  with the perpendicular of  $\overline{AC}$ , which passes through  $+\sqrt{ac}$  and  $-\sqrt{ac}$ , we calculate  $E=\epsilon$  as  $$ \frac{bx(+\sqrt{ac}-\sqrt{ac})-(+\sqrt{ac})(-\sqrt{ac})(b+x)}{bx-(+\sqrt{ac})(-\sqrt{ac})}=\frac{ac(b+x)}{bx+ac}. $$  To demonstrate the tangency it suffices to show that
\begin{align*}
\frac{\epsilon-\tfrac{b+c}{2}}{\epsilon-b} \div \frac{b-c}{b-a} &\in \mathbb{R} &\iff
\frac{(2ac(b+x)-(b+c)(bx+ac))(b-a)}{(ac(b+x)-b(bx+ac))(b-c)} &\in \mathbb{R} &\iff
\frac{((2ac-b^2-bc)x+(abc-ac^2))(b-a)}{x(ac-b^2)(b-c)} &\in \mathbb{R} &\iff
((2ac-b^2-bc)x+(abc-ac^2))(b-a)\frac{1}{x}\left(\frac{1}{ac}-\frac{1}{b^2}\right)\left(\frac{1}{b}-\frac{1}{c}\right) &= \left(\left(\frac{2}{ac}-\frac{1}{b^2}-\frac{1}{bc}\right)\frac{1}{x}+\left(\frac{1}{abc}-\frac{1}{ac^2}\right)\right)\left(\frac{1}{b}-\frac{1}{a}\right)x(ac-b^2)(b-c).
\end{align*}
Note that  $\tfrac{1}{ac}-\tfrac{1}{b^2}=-\tfrac{ac-b^2}{ab^2c}$ ,  $\tfrac{1}{b}-\tfrac{1}{c}=-\tfrac{b-c}{bc}$ , and  $\tfrac{1}{b}-\tfrac{1}{a}=-\tfrac{b-a}{ab}$ . Upon cancellation and multiplying through by  $x$  the above equation rewrites as  $$ -((2ac-b^2-bc)x+(abc-ac^2))\frac{1}{ab^2c}\frac{1}{bc}=x\left(\left(\frac{1}{abc}-\frac{1}{ac^2}\right)x+\left(\frac{2}{ac}-\frac{1}{b^2}-\frac{1}{bc}\right)\right)\frac{1}{ab}. $$  Moving terms to one side and multiplying by  $a^2b^3c^2$  yields  $$ (b^2-bc)x^2-(2b^2c-ac^2-abc)x-(2a^2c-ab^2-abc)x-(a^2bc-a^2c^2)=0 \iff b(b-c)x^2+(a(b^2+c^2+2bc-2ac)-2b^2c)x+a^2c(c-b)=0, $$  which is our original condition, so  $(BEM)$  is indeed tangent to  $\overline{AB}$ .  $\blacksquare$

As the circle tangent to  $AB$  at  $B$  passing through  $M$  is unique, as well as points  $E$  and  $D$ . We shall consider the following rephrasing of the question:

There exists line  $AB$ , and a circle  $\omega$  tangent to  $AB$  at  $B$ . We mark  $M$  to be a point on the circle and  $C$  to be the reflection of  $B$  across  $M$ . Then consider the perpendicular bisector  $AC$  and let it intersect  $\omega$  at  $E$ . finally consider line  $l$  that passes through A such that the angle  $\angle (l,AC) = \angle ACB$ . We consider  $D$  to be intersection of  $l$  and line  $BE$ .  Show that  $\angle BDC = 90^{\circ} + \angle BAC$ Define  $K$  to be intersection of  $AD$  with  $BC$ .

We perform a  $\sqrt{ac}$  inversion with reflection about angle bisector. We no longer worry about the original figure, hence all points are named as per their original names while  $P^*$  refers to point  $P$  in the original figure , with  $A$  and  $C$  interchanged (As  $A$  maps to  $C$  and vice versa in this transformation.) We call  $C'$  as the reflection of  $C$  across  $A$ . Now note, as  $M$  was midpoint of  $BC$  it now maps to reflection of  $B$  across  $A$ , hence  $CMC'B$  form a parallelogram. However  $\omega$  maps to line through  $M$  parallel to  $BC$  hence  $C'$  lies on  $\omega$ .  Now as triangle  $BAC$  is preserved hence let  $\angle BCA = C$ . Now in the original figure  $\angle BKA = 2C$  hence  $\angle BCK = 2C$ . Note this implies  $CA$  is angle bisector of  $\angle BCK$  This will be important. Let  $I$  be incenter of  $BCK$ . 

