Claim:  $BIEK$  is cyclic.
Proof: now note  $\frac{AE}{CE}= \frac{\frac{C^*E^*ac}{BC^*BE^*}}{\frac{A^*E^*ac}{BA^*BE^*}} = \frac{BA^*}{BC^*}$ similarly  $\frac{AK}{CK}= \frac{\frac{C^*K^*ac}{BC^*BK^*}}{\frac{A^*K^*ac}{BA^*BK^*}} = \frac{BA^*}{BC^*}$ meanwhile  $\frac{AB}{CB} = \frac{\frac{ac}{BC^*}}{\frac{ac}{BA^*}} = \frac{BA^*}{BC^*}. $ we also know  $I$  lies on angle bisector of  $\angle CBK = \angle CBA$  hence  $\frac{AI}{CI} = \frac{AB}{CB} = \frac{BA^*}{BC^*}.$ Hence all 4 points lie on a common apollonius circle. conclude claim.

Now as  $A^*-D^*-K^*$  are collinear  $CDKB$  are concyclic hence  $\angle KDB = \angle KCB = 2C$ .
However  $\angle KEB  = \angle KIB = 90+C$ . Hence  $\angle KED = 90-C$  ie.  $\angle EKD = 90-C$  or  $ED = DK$ .
Let  $N$  be  $CK$  intersection  $\omega$ . we have then  $\angle KNE = \angle CNC' = 180 - \angle BCN = 180-2C$ . Hence  $NKDE$  is cyclic.

From here, note  $D$  lies on angle bisector of  $KNE$  as  $D$  is midpoint of arc  $KE$ . let the angle bisector extend to meet  $CC'$  at Point  $A'$ . However now note  $\angle NA'C = 90$  which means the angle bisector is also the perpendicular from  $N$  onto  $CC'$ . Hence We have that it must be the perpendicular bisector as well. Hence,  $D$  lies on perpendicular bisector of  $CC'$ . However the condition we needed to show was  $\angle BAD = 90^{\circ} + \angle BAC$  or  $\angle CAD = 90^{\circ}$  which is now clear



