We claim the only values of  $n$  that work are  $n=\boxed{3,4}.$ Let the greatest prime greater than  $n$  be  $p.$  By Bertrand's Postulate,  $n<p<2n-2$  meaning that  $p-2,p-1,p+1$  all divide  $n!.$  We then have consecutive divisors of  $d_{i-1},d_{i},d_{i+1}$  being  $\frac{n!}{p+1}, \frac{n!}{p-1}, \frac{n!}{p-2}.$  But then, 
\begin{align*}
d_{i}-d_{i-1} &\le d_{i+1}-d_{i} 
\frac{n!}{p+1}-\frac{n!}{p-1} &\le \frac{n!}{p-1}-\frac{n!}{p-2} 
\frac{2}{p^2-1} &\le \frac{1}{p^2-3p+2} 
2p^2-6p+4 &\le p^2-1 
p^2-6p+5 &\le 0  
(p-1)(p-5) &\le 0  
\end{align*}

implying that no  $n \ge 5$  work.  $\blacksquare$

