This is too easy with complex numbers; I barely even tried any synthetic.

Label  $a = x^2$ ,  $b = y^2$ ,  $c = z^2$ , and let  $j = -(xy+yz+zx)$  denote the incenter, as usual. The given condition implies that  $\arg\left(\tfrac{p-a}{p-j}\right) = \frac{\pi}4$  which simplifies to \[\frac{p-x^2}{(p+xy+yz+zx)(1+i)} = \frac{\frac 1p - \frac 1{x^2}}{\left(\frac 1p+\frac 1{xy}+\frac 1{yz}+\frac 1{zx}\right)}.\] Some similar computation with  $q$  yields 
\begin{align*}
p &= \frac{yz(1+i)(xy+yz+zx) + x^2yz(1-i)}{x(i-1)(x+y+z)-yz(1+i)} 
q &= \frac{yz(1+i)(xy+yz+zx)-x^2yz(1-i)}{x(1-i)(x+y+z)-yz(1+i)}.
\end{align*}
It is not hard to compute now 
\begin{align*}
p+q &= -\frac{2yz\left(x^4+x^3(y+z)+xyz(y+z)+y^2z^2\right)}{y^2z^2+x^2(x+y+z)^2} 
pq &= \frac{y^2z^2\left(x^4+(xy+yz+zx)^2\right)}{y^2z^2+x^2(x+y+z)^2}.
\end{align*}
The chord intersection formula yields 
\begin{align*}
s &= \frac{pq\left(y^2+z^2\right)-y^2z^2(p+q)}{pq-y^2z^2} 
&= \frac{x^4(y+z)+2x^3yz+x^2y^3+x^2z^3+x^2y^2z+x^2yz^2+2xy^3z+2xyz^3+2xy^2z^2+y^3z^2+y^2z^3}{2x\left(yz-x^2\right)}.
\end{align*}
The angle  $\angle ISH$  can be represented by \[\frac{S-j}{y^2-z^2} = \frac{(x+y)(x+z)\left(x^2-xy-xz-yz\right)}{-2x(y-z)\left(yz-x^2\right)}\] and for the foot of the altitude  $d = \tfrac 12\left(x^2+y^2+z^2+\tfrac{y^2z^2}{x^2}\right)$ , the angle  $\angle AHI$  is represented by \[\frac{d-a}{d-j} = \frac{(x-y)(x+y)(z-x)(x+z)}{x^4+x^3(y+z)+x^2yz+2\left(x^2y^2+y^2z^2+z^2x^2\right)} = \frac{x^2+xy+xz-yz}{(x-y)(z-x)}.\] The quotient of these two angles is then given by \[r = \frac{\left(xy+xz+yz-x^2\right)(x+y)(x+z)\left(x^2+xy+xz-yz\right)}{2x(y-z)(x-y)(z-x)\left(yz-x^2\right)}.\] Everything here is self-conjugate except for conjugate pairs  $\left(x^2-xy-xz-yz, x^2+xy+xz-yz\right)$ , and there are a total of  $4$  flipped signs (for each term in the denominator), so this quantity is real, and thus the two angles must be equal.

