<span style="color:#00f">**Claim**</span>:  $\overline{K-D-M}$ .
<span style="color:#f00">*Proof*</span>: Inversion about  $M$  with radius  $MI$  shows  $K$  and  $D$  swap with each other, showing that  $K,D,M$  are collinear.  $\square$ <span style="color:#00f">**Claim**</span>:  $\overline{K-W-D}$ .
<span style="color:#f00">*Proof*</span>: Let  $T \equiv AK \cap BC$ . As  $(AI)$  and  $(BIC)$  are tangent at  $I$ , by radical axis theorem in  $(AI)$ ,  $(BIC)$  and  $(ABC)$ , we know  $TI$  is tangent to  $(AI)$ . Hence, \[\angle AIT=\angle AHT=90^\circ\]which shows  $AHTI$  is cyclic. Now, we have  $\angle IWH=\angle IDH=IKT=90^\circ$ , which shows  $K,W,D$  are collinear by Simson lines.  $\square$ Now, by these lemmas,  $\angle AHI=\angle ISH$  if and only if  $\triangle IDS \sim \triangle IWD$ . Then, it suffices to show that  $KM \perp IS$ , after which we will be done.

We perform incircle inversion and it suffices to solve the following problem(where the notations are kept the same):
<blockquote>Let a  $\triangle ABC$  with orthocenter  $I$ .  $P$  and  $Q$  are two points on  $(ABC)$ , on the opposite sides of  $AI$ , such that  $\angle IAP=\angle IAQ=45^\circ$ . Let  $M \equiv AI \cap (ABC)(\neq A)$  and  $K$  be the antipode of  $M$  with respect to  $(ABC)$ . Let  $(IPQ) \cap (IBC) \equiv S(\neq I)$ . Prove that  $IS$  is orthogonal to  $(IKM)$ , that is,  $IS$  passes through the circumcenter of  $\triangle IKM$ .</blockquote>
Note that  $PQ$  is a diameter in  $(ABC)$  and as  $\angle IAP=\angle IAQ=45^\circ$ , we have that  $PMQK$  is a square. By radical axis theorem on  $(BISC)$ ,  $(PISQ)$  and  $(PKQM)$ , we have  $IS, PQ$  and  $BC$  concur at a point  $X$ .
Notice  $X$  lies on the perpendicular bisector of  $MK$  as well as on the perpendicular bisector of  $IM$ (as  $M$  is the reflection of  $I$  about  $BC$ ), which implies  $X$  is indeed, the circumcenter of  $\triangle MKI$ , and lies on line  $IS$ .

Inverting back, we get  $KM \perp IS$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$ <details><summary>remarks</summary>Nice problem, I was thinking for a lot of time even after trying naturally motivated inversion and making use of sharkydevil config, but the fact that it suffices to show  $KM \perp IS$  made everything fall together.</details>

The answer is  $250500$  moves.**<span style="color:blue">Construction:**</span> Perform the following moves in succession:


- Remove one stone from  $(1000, 1001)$ ;
- Remove one stone from  $(999, 1001)$  and one from  $(999, 1000)$ ;
- In general, in lexicographically decreasing order of  $(i, j)$ , remove one stone from  $(i, j)$  for each  $501 \leq i \leq 1001$  and  $i+1 \leq j \leq 1001$ .
- For  $1 \leq i \leq 500$ , perform the above sequence in lexicographically decreasing order until pile  $i$  runs out of stones, then move to pile  $i-1$ .

I claim that, whenever we perform move  $(i, j)$  above, there is always exactly one more stone in pile  $j$  than pile  $i$ . Indeed, up to this point, pile  $j$  loses one stone for each index  $i+1 \leq k \leq 1001$  that is not  $j$ , so it contains  $i + j - 999$  stones. Pile  $i$  loses one stone for each index  $j+1 \leq k \leq 1001$ , so it contains  $i + j - 1000$  stones. Hence the above sequence is valid, and it generates \[2(1+2+3+\cdots+500) = 500 \cdot 501 = 250500\] moves.**<span style="color:blue">Bound:**</span> Any move can be classified as a *big move* between two piles with indexes  $i, j \geq 501$  or a *small move* otherwise. But since each  $(i, j)$  is chosen at most once, there are at most  $\tbinom{501}2$  big moves, and since there are at most  $\tbinom{501}2$  stones in the small piles, there are at most  $\tbinom{501}2$  small moves. So there can be at most  $500 \cdot 501 = 250500$  moves in total.

