Thanks to Popop and everyone else for proofreading.

------------------------------------
Note that the distance relation is symmetric. As such, we can first do the following.  
- Take each knight on the board and remove it, coloring the square it was on green.
- Put some brand new knights on the board.
- Note that each brand new knight put in any cell attacks an even number of green squares.
 Let  $a_{i,j}$  be the indicator variable for whether  $(i, j)$  is green. Then the above means that for any knight, the sum of the eight indicator variables  $a_{i \pm 1, j \pm 2}, a_{i \pm 2, j \pm 1}$  is zero. Extend our indicator variable to all of  $\mathbb{Z}^2$  for conciseness. When one of  $i, j$  is not in  $[1, n]$ , we require only that  $a_{ij} = a_{n+1-i,j} = a_{i,n+1-j}$  for our first claim, and take  $a_{ij} = 0$  for our second.**<span style="color:pink">Claim:</span>** Given the above constraints on  $a_{ij}$ , we can show that  $a_{1,j} = a_{n,j}$  and  $a_{j,1} = a_{j,n}$  for all  $j$ .
*Proof.* Consider taking knights in the following construction in the  $j$ th row (here's the example for  $n = 9$ ,  $j = 3$ ).
[asy] size(10cm);
int W=9; int H=9; for (int i=0; i<=W; ++i) { draw( (i,0)--(i,H), rgb("333333")+dotted ); } for (int i=0; i<=H; ++i) { draw( (0,i)--(W,i), rgb("333333")+dotted ); }
for (int i=0; i <= 4; ++i) { label(" $N$ ", (0.5+2*i, 2.5), (0,0)); filldraw(shift(2*i, 2)*unitsquare, red+white); } filldraw(shift(0, 1)*unitsquare, green+white); filldraw(shift(0, 3)*unitsquare, green+white); filldraw(shift(8, 1)*unitsquare, green+white); filldraw(shift(8, 3)*unitsquare, green+white);
filldraw(shift(-1, 0)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(-1, 4)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(9, 0)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(9, 4)*unitsquare, purple+white);
filldraw(shift(-2, 1)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(-2, 3)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(10, 1)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(10, 3)*unitsquare, purple+white);
[/asy]
Here, the four green cells are all the cells appear an odd number of times in the grid. The purple cells indicate those that appear an odd number of times but are outside the grid (and thus we can pair up and don't affect our sum). The  $N$ s are a configuration of knights.
Using the knight sum assertion and summing over our knights, we get that for any  $j$ , the sum \[ a_{1,j+1} + a_{1,j-1} + a_{n,j+1} + a_{n,j-1} \equiv 0 \pmod{2} \quad (\clubsuit). \] Call this assertion  $(\clubsuit)$ .
Furthermore, by considering  $j=1$ , we obtain that  $a_{1,2} = a_{n,2}$ . Using the above relation  $(\clubsuit)$  we obtain that  $a_{1,2k} = a_{n,2k}$  for all  $k$ .  We use the following second construction (this is the example for  $n = 13$ ) which is made by taking frames of width  $2$  (or center  $1$ ) and filling every other as a checkerboard.   [asy] size(10cm);
int W=14; int H=14; for (int i=1; i<=W; ++i) { draw( (i,0)--(i,H-1), rgb("333333")+dotted ); } for (int i=0; i<=H-1; ++i) { draw( (1,i)--(W,i), rgb("333333")+dotted ); }
for (int i=0; i<=W; ++i) { for (int j=0; j<=H; ++j) { int t = min(min(i, W-i), min(j,H-j)); if (t % 4 == 2 || t % 4 == 3) { if ((i+j) % 2 == 0) { label(" $N$ ", (0.5+i, 0.5+j), (0,0)); filldraw(shift(i, j)*unitsquare, red+white); } } } }
filldraw(shift(1,0)*unitsquare, green+white); filldraw(shift(0,1)*unitsquare, purple+white);
filldraw(shift(2+W-2,1)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(1+W-2,0)*unitsquare, green+white);
filldraw(shift(0,1+H-2)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(1,2+H-2)*unitsquare, purple+white);
filldraw(shift(2+W-2,1+H-2)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(1+W-2,2+H-2)*unitsquare, purple+white);  [/asy]  which generalizes for all odd  $k$ . This means that we get that  $a_{1,n} \equiv a_{n,n} \pmod{2}$ .  Combining our assertions gives the first claim. By symmetry, we get the second claim.  $\blacksquare$ However, now we can induct down on  $n$  considering the smaller grid  $[2,n-1] \times [2,n-1]$ . This finishes the horizontal and vertical axis by inducting down to  $n = 1$ . We now prove the result for diagonals.**<span style="color:pink">Claim:</span>** We have that  $a_{i,j} = a_{j,i}$  and likewise for the other diagonal reflecting.
*Proof.* Suppose we reflect about the bottom left to top right diagonal. We first consider diagonals with odd distance between them.
By our second construction from the previous claim the result for the squares which have distance  $1$  to their reflection (We take the construction in both the top right and bottom left corner to cover all such possibilities).    [asy] size(10cm);
int W=10; int H=10; for (int i=-2; i<=W-1; ++i) { draw( (i,-2)--(i,H-1), rgb("333333")+dotted ); } for (int i=-2; i<=H-1; ++i) { draw( (-2,i)--(W-1,i), rgb("333333")+dotted ); }
for (int i=0; i<=W; ++i) { for (int j=0; j<=H; ++j) { int t = min(min(i, W-i), min(j,H-j)); if (t % 4 == 2 || t % 4 == 3) { if ((i+j) % 2 == 0) { label(" $N$ ", (0.5+i, 0.5+j), (0,0)); filldraw(shift(i, j)*unitsquare, red+white); } } } }
filldraw(shift(1,0)*unitsquare, green+white); filldraw(shift(0,1)*unitsquare, green+white);
filldraw(shift(2+W-2,1)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(1+W-2,0)*unitsquare, purple+white);
filldraw(shift(0,1+H-2)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(1,2+H-2)*unitsquare, purple+white);
filldraw(shift(2+W-2,1+H-2)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(1+W-2,2+H-2)*unitsquare, purple+white);  [/asy]
We now inductively reduce to this case. Suppose the result for holds all odd distances  $\le k$ . We show it hold for a cell  $c$  whose reflection  $c'$  is  $k+2$  apart.
The idea is to place some knights in diagonals closer to and symmetric about the reflecting diagonal such that the cells with an odd number of knights attacking them are exactly  $c$  and some other cells with a smaller distance to the center.
Here's the construction for a specific  $c$  colored in blue, but it generalizes easily as the farther away diagonals are smaller lengths. The purple cells cancel out inductively and thus the blue cells sum to zero  $\pmod{2}$ .
[asy] size(10cm);
int W=9; int H=9; for (int i=0; i<=W; ++i) { draw( (i,0)--(i,H), rgb("333333")+dotted ); } for (int i=0; i<=H; ++i) { draw( (0,i)--(W,i), rgb("333333")+dotted ); }
for (int i=0; i <= 4; ++i) { int t = 2+i; int r = 4+i; label(" $N$ ", (0.5+t, 0.5+r), (0,0)); filldraw(shift(t, r)*unitsquare, red+white); label(" $N$ ", (0.5+t+2, 0.5+r-2), (0,0)); filldraw(shift(t+2, r-2)*unitsquare, red+white); }
filldraw(shift(0,5)*unitsquare, blue+white); filldraw(shift(5,0)*unitsquare, blue+white); label(" $c$ ", (0.5, 5.5), (0,0)); label(" $c'$ ", (5.5, 0.5), (0,0));
filldraw(shift(0,3)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(1,2)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(2,1)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(3,0)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(1,4)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(2,3)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(3,2)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(4,1)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(3,4)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(4,3)*unitsquare, purple+white);
filldraw(shift(5,8)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(6,7)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(7,6)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(8,5)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(8,7)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(7,8)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(6,9)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(9,6)*unitsquare, purple+white);
[/asy]   The odd diagonal case is a bit more complicated. We can generalize our second construction to work for the diagonal of length  $3$  at the edge, call this our third construction.
[asy] size(10cm);
int W=11; int H=11; for (int i=-2; i<=W-2; ++i) { draw( (i,-2)--(i,H-2), rgb("333333")+dotted ); } for (int i=-2; i<=H-2; ++i) { draw( (-2,i)--(W-2,i), rgb("333333")+dotted ); }
int[][] points = { {0,1}, {0,3}, {0,5}, {0,7}, {1,8}, {3,8}, {5,8}, {7,8}, {1,0}, {3,0}, {5,0}, {7,0}, {8,1}, {8,3}, {8,5}, {8,7}, {3,2}, {5,2}, {6,3}, {6,5}, {2,3}, {2,5}, {3,6}, {5,6} };
for (int i=0; i <= 8; ++i) { for (int j=0; j <= 8; ++j) { for (int k=0; k < points.length; ++k) { if (points[k][0] == i && points[k][1] == j) { label(" $N$ ", (0.5+i, 0.5+j), (0,0)); filldraw(shift(i, j)*unitsquare, red+white); break; } } } }
int N = 8;
filldraw(shift(0,-2)*unitsquare, green+white); filldraw(shift(-2,0)*unitsquare, green+white); filldraw(shift(N,-2)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(N+2,0)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(0,N+2)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(-2,N+0)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(N+0,N+2)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(N+2,N+0)*unitsquare, purple+white);  [/asy]
Here, we instead induct on going from the bottom right corner in squares using knights similar to above. Given the pink as a known square case, we can induct up to the next square by doing the following with our third construction to get  $c, c'$  (where the purple squares pair up either since they are out of bounds or they reflect to a known case), then covering the remaining cells in the larger square.
[asy] size(10cm);
int W=9; int H=9; for (int i=0; i<=W; ++i) { draw( (i,0)--(i,H), rgb("333333")+dotted ); } for (int i=0; i<=H; ++i) { draw( (0,i)--(W,i), rgb("333333")+dotted ); }
int[][] points = { {4,5}, {5,4}, {7,4}, {4,7}, {5,8}, {8,5}, {7,8}, {8,7} };
for (int i=0; i <= W+1; ++i) { for (int j=0; j <= H+1; ++j) { for (int k=0; k < points.length; ++k) { if (points[k][0] == i && points[k][1] == j) { label(" $N$ ", (0.5+i, 0.5+j), (0,0)); filldraw(shift(i, j)*unitsquare, red+white); break; } } } }
filldraw(shift(2,4)*unitsquare, blue+white); filldraw(shift(4,2)*unitsquare, blue+white); filldraw(shift(10,4)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(2,8)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(10,8)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(4,10)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(8,2)*unitsquare, purple+white); filldraw(shift(8,10)*unitsquare, purple+white);
label(" $c$ ", (2.5, 4.5), (0,0)); label(" $c'$ ", (4.5, 2.5), (0,0));
filldraw(shift(1,1)*unitsquare, pink); filldraw(shift(1,3)*unitsquare, pink); filldraw(shift(2,2)*unitsquare, pink); filldraw(shift(3,1)*unitsquare, pink); filldraw(shift(0,0)*unitsquare, pink); filldraw(shift(0,2)*unitsquare, pink); filldraw(shift(2,0)*unitsquare, pink); filldraw(shift(3,3)*unitsquare, pink); filldraw(shift(4,4)*unitsquare, pink); filldraw(shift(5,5)*unitsquare, pink); filldraw(shift(6,6)*unitsquare, pink); filldraw(shift(7,7)*unitsquare, pink); filldraw(shift(8,8)*unitsquare, pink);
[/asy]   $\blacksquare$

