We claim that the only such sequence is  $1,0,0,\dots$ , which works since then  $-n$  is a root of  $1+x^{a_1}+x^{a_2}+\dots+x^{a_n}$ . We will now show that this is the only possible sequence; consider any working  $\{a_n\}_{n\geq 1}$ . Let  $-r_n$  be a root of  $1+x^{a_1}+x^{a_2}+\dots+x^{a_n}$ . Note that  $r_n$  is always a positive integer. We now have \[n+1\equiv 1+(-r_n)^{a_1}+(-r_n)^{a_2}+\dots+(-r_n)^{a_n}\equiv 0\pmod {r_n+1}\implies r_n+1\mid n+1\] It follows that when  $n+1$  is prime,  $r_n=n$ . Now consider any  $n>1$  with  $r_n=n$ . Let  $m$  be the largest value among  $a_1,a_2,\dots,a_n$ , and suppose it occurs  $k$  times. Then
\begin{align*}
&1+(-n)^{a_1}+(-n)^{a_2}+\dots+(-n)^{a_n}=0
\implies &\left|1+\sum_{a_i<m}(-n)^{a_i}\right|=kn^m
\implies &1+\sum_{a_i<m}n^{a_i}\geq kn^m
\implies &1+(n-k)n^{m-1}\geq kn^m
\implies &\frac{1}{kn^m}\geq k-1+\frac{k}{n}
\implies &(k,m)=(1,1)
\end{align*}
Then  $(a_1,a_2,\dots,a_n)$  is a permutation of  $(1,\underbrace{0,\dots,0}_{n-1})$ . But since  $1+x^{a_1}$  has an integer root, we must have  $(a_1,a_2,\dots,a_n)=(1,\underbrace{0,\dots,0}_{n-1})$ . Now since  $r_n=n$  infinitely often, the sequence  $\{a_n\}_{n\geq 1}$  must be exactly \[1,0,0,\dots\] as desired.

