Write  $z_1\sim z_2$  for the equivalence relation  $z_1/z_2\in\mathbb R\setminus\{0\}$ . The condition that  $X\in(EFG)$  is equivalent to  $$ \frac{e-x}{f-x}\sim\frac{e-g}{f-g}\sim\frac{b-e}{c-f}, $$  while the condition  $\angle BGC=\angle BXC+\angle EDF$  is equivalent to  $$ \frac{b-x}{c-x}\sim\frac{b-g}{c-g}\cdot\frac{d-f}{d-e}\sim\frac{(b-e)(d-f)}{(c-f)(d-e)}. $$  These both define circles, which we call  $\Omega_1$  and  $\Omega_2$ , and we must show that the circles are tangent. The idea is to write both as Möbius transformations of the real line.**Lemma.** Given points  $p$  and  $q$  and complex numbers  $\theta_1$  and  $\theta_2$ , the circle through  $p$  and  $q$  described by  $\frac{p-z}{q-z}\sim\frac{\theta_1}{\theta_2}$  is parametrized by  $$ \left\{\frac{\theta_1qt+\theta_2p}{\theta_1t+\theta_2} : t\in\mathbb R\cup\{\infty\}\right\}. $$  *Proof.* This parametrization certainly defines a circle. For any point  $r$  on this circle, we have some  $t\in\mathbb R\cup\{\infty\}$  for which  $$ \frac{r-p}{r-q}=\frac{(\theta_1qt+\theta_2p)-p(\theta_1t+\theta_2)}{(\theta_1qt+\theta_2p)-q(\theta_1t+\theta_2)}=\frac{\theta_1t(q-p)}{\theta_2(p-q)}=-t\frac{\theta_1}{\theta_2}\sim\frac{\theta_1}{\theta_2}, $$  as desired. :yoda:

So, we can write  $$ \Omega_1=\left\{\frac{(b-e)ft+(c-f)e}{(b-e)t+(c-f)} : t\in\mathbb R\cup\infty\right\},\qquad \Omega_2=\left\{\frac{(b-e)(d-f)cu+(c-f)(d-e)b}{(b-e)(d-f)t+(c-f)(d-e)} : u\in\mathbb R\cup\infty\right\}. $$  We must show that these circles are tangent. Define  $\alpha_1,\beta_1,\gamma_1,\delta_1,\alpha_2,\beta_2,\gamma_2,\delta_2$  so that, with the above parametrization,  $\Omega_i=\left\{\frac{\alpha_it+\beta_i}{\gamma_it+\delta_i}:t\in\mathbb R\cup\{\infty\}\right\}$ . We may now equate the parametrized points on both circles and solve for  $t$  in terms of  $u$ ; our goal is to show that there is exactly one  $t$  for which  $t$  and  $u$  are real.
\begin{align*}
\frac{\alpha_2u+\beta_2}{\gamma_2u+\delta_2}&=x=\frac{\alpha_1t+\beta_1}{\gamma_1t+\delta_1}\implies
\alpha_1t+\beta_1&=x\gamma_1t+x\delta_1
t&=\frac{\beta_1-x\delta_1}{x\gamma_1-\alpha_1}=\frac{\beta_1(\gamma_2u+\delta_2)-\delta_1(\alpha_2u+\beta_2)}{\gamma_1(\alpha_2u+\beta_2)-\alpha_1(\gamma_2u+\delta_2)}
&=\frac{u(\beta_1\gamma_2-\delta_1\alpha_2)+(\beta_1\delta_2-\delta_1\beta_2)}{u(\gamma_1\alpha_2-\alpha_1\gamma_2)+(\gamma_1\beta_2-\alpha_1\delta_2)}.
\end{align*}
We compute
\begin{align*}
\beta_1\gamma_2-\delta_1\alpha_2&=(b-e)(c-f)(d-e)(b-f)
\beta_1\delta_2-\delta_1\beta_2&=(b-e)(c-f)^2(d-e)
\gamma_1\alpha_2-\alpha_1\gamma_2&=-(b-e)^2(c-f)(d-f)
\beta_2\gamma_1-\alpha_1\delta_2&=-(b-e)(c-f)(d-f)(c-e).
\end{align*}
Therefore, it suffices to show that there is exactly one  $u\in\mathbb R\cup\{\infty\}$  for which  $$ h(u):=\frac{d-e}{d-f}\cdot\frac{(b-f)u+(c-f)}{(b-e)u+(c-e)}\in\mathbb R\cup\{\infty\}. $$  We remark that, since  $D$  lies on segment  $BC$ , there exists some  $\lambda\in\mathbb R\setminus\{0,1\}$  for which  $b\lambda+c(1-\lambda)=d$ . Setting  $u=u_0:=\lambda/(1-\lambda)$ , we have  $h(u)=1$ . Therefore, we must show that  $h(u)$  is real if and only if  $u=\lambda/(1-\lambda)$ . We translate  $h$ :  $$ h(v+u_0)=\frac{d-e}{d-f}\cdot\frac{(b-f)v+(b-f)u_0+(c-f)}{(b-e)v+(b-e)u_0+(b-f)}=\frac{d-e}{d-f}\cdot\frac{(1-\lambda)(b-f)v+(d-f)}{(1-\lambda)(b-e)v+(d-e)}; $$  equivalently,
\begin{align*}
\tilde h(w):=h\left(\frac w{1-\lambda}+u_0\right)-1
&=\frac{d-e}{d-f}\cdot\frac{(b-f)w+(d-f)}{(b-e)w+(d-e)}-1
&=\frac{\frac{b-f}{d-f}w+1}{\frac{b-e}{d-e}w+1}-1=\frac{\left(\frac{b-f}{d-f}-\frac{b-e}{d-e}\right)w}{\frac{b-e}{d-e}w+1}.
\end{align*}
It suffices to show that  $\tilde h(w)$  is real if and only if  $w=0$ . Since  $\frac{b-e}{d-e}\not\in\mathbb R$  as  $B$ ,  $E$ , and  $D$  are not collinear, it suffices to show that  $\frac{b-f}{d-f}-\frac{b-e}{d-e}\in\mathbb R$ . Indeed, we have  $$ \frac{b-f}{d-f}-\frac{b-e}{d-e}=\frac{b-d}{d-f}-\frac{b-d}{d-e}=\frac{(b-d)(f-e)}{(d-e)(d-f)}=\frac{\frac{b-d}{d-e}}{\frac{d-f}{f-e}}, $$  which is real since  $\measuredangle BDE=\measuredangle FDE$  as  $(DEF)$  and  $BD$  are tangent.

