We will now show that we cannot have more than  $44$  pleasant moves. If the king moves from  $(i_1, j_1)$  to  $(i_2, j_2)$ , we write  $(i_1, j_1) \to (i_2, j_2)$ .  Partition the board into four  $4 \times 4$  boards. Consider the top-left one.

Clearly, the move arriving at  $(1, 1)$  is unpleasant. Similarly, the move arriving at  $(1, 2)$  and  $(2, 1)$  is unpleasant as long as the king wasn't at  $(1, 1)$  beforehand. In particular, at least one of these moves is unpleasant.
Now, suppose that the moves arriving at cells  $(1, 3)$ ,  $(1, 4)$ ,  $(3, 1)$ ,  $(4, 1)$  are all pleasant. We may hand-check that  $(2, 1) \to (3, 1)\to (4, 1)$ ,  $(1, 2) \to (1, 3) \to (1, 4)$ , and  $(2, 2) \to (1, 1)$ . It is easy to check that the move arriving at  $(2, 2)$  is unpleasant giving us  $3$  unpleasant moves in total.

<details><summary>Diagram</summary>[asy]
size(4cm, 4cm);
