This (together with Step  $2$ ) essentially implies that all chord lengths of a given parity are present in the cycle, where we define the chord length as the size of the smaller arc it makes (although the definition would also work for the larger arc).

If  $n$  is odd, the smaller and larger arcs differ in parity, and thus if one chord is present, the graph must be complete.

If  $n$  is even and we suppose that chords of even length exist, in particular chords of length  $2$ , then consider the path  $v_1v_3v_2v_nv_{n-1}\dots v_5v_4$ . This path is Hamiltonian, so by Property  $(*)$ ,  $v_1$  must be adjacent to  $v_4$ . Thus, a chord of length  $3$  exists, which implies that  $G$  must be complete. If only the odd chords are present, notice that  $G$  is complete bipartite with components  $\{v_1,v_3,\dots,v_{n-1}\}$  and  $\{v_2,v_4,\dots,v_n\}$ , or equivalently  $G=K_{n/2,n/2}$  which we have established to work.  $\square$ **Remarks:** The idea in Step 1 is quite standard and natural given the context of the problem. Step 2 follows by playing around with the configurations, while Step 3 is strongly motivated by the structure of equality cases when  $n$  is even. Not very difficult, but definitely a fun one!

