(**The official solution**) For each  $i=1,2,\ldots, n$  let  $E_i$  be the event that all points lie within the arc  $\mathcal{C}_i$  of length  $1/m,$  starting from  $A_i$  in the clockwise direction. We have that for each point  $A_j, j\neq i$ ,  $\mathbb{P}(A_j \in \mathcal{C}_i)=1/m$ , and since we choose  $A_i$  independently, it follows that  $\mathbb{P}(E_i) = \frac{1}{m^{n-1}}.$  Note also that since  $\frac{2}{m}\leq 1$ , then  $\mathbb{P}(E_i\cap E_j) = 0$  for  $i\neq j.$  Therefore 
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{P}\left(\bigcup_{i=1}^n E_i\right) = \sum\limits_{i=1}^n \mathbb{P}(E_i) = \frac{n}{m^{n-1}}
\end{align*}**Here is another approach**, using**[b]**[/b] the coupling technique. If  $t$  is a point on the circle, and  $c\in \mathbb{R}$ , then  $t+c$  denotes the point on the circle rotated by  $c$  radians from  $t$ . Let  $X$  be the set of all tuples  $x=(x_1,x_2,\ldots, x_n)$  of  $n$  points on the circle. For  $x\in X$ , define:  $$ S_x:=\{y=(y_1,\ldots, y_n) : y_i=x_i+k_i/m,\, k_i=0,1,\ldots,m-1,\, i=1,2,\ldots,n\}. $$  Note that for any two  $x,y\in X$ , either  $S_x=S_y$  or  $S_x\cap S_y=\emptyset$ , i.e.,  $X$  is partitioned into classes of subsets, each consisting of  $m^n$  elements. It remains to observe that for a fixed class  $S_x$ , exactly  $n\cdot m$  of the tuples in it lie on an arc of length  $1/m$ . Thus the desired probability is  $\frac{nm}{m^n}=\frac{n}{m^{n-1}}$ .

