Let  $N$  be the arc midpoint of  $BC$  containing  $A$ . The following claims finish the problem: $\textbf{Claim 1: }$   $Z, A, N$  are collinear.
*Proof.* Let the angle bisector of  $\angle BAC$  intersect  $\overline{XY}$  at  $T$ , note that  $AT$  bisects  $\angle XAY$ . Define  $r_{BAE}$  and  $r_{CAD}$  as the inradii of  $\triangle BAE$  and  $\triangle CAD$  respectively. By homothety we have
\[ \dfrac{ZX}{ZY} = \dfrac{r_{BAE}}{r_{CAD}} = \dfrac{r_{BAE} \csc(\angle BAE / 2)}{r_{CAD} \csc(\angle CAD / 2)} = \dfrac{AX}{AY} = \dfrac{XT}{TY} \]
but  $(Z, T ; X, Y) = \dfrac{XZ}{XT} \cdot \dfrac{YT}{YZ} = -1$  so  $(Z, T; X, Y)$  is a harmonic bundle. Then  $ZA \perp AT$ , implying  $Z, A, N,$  are collinear. $\textbf{Claim 2: }$   $N \in (AXY)$ .
*Proof.* We employ complex numbers. WLOG  $(ABC)$  is the unit circle, let  $A, B, E \equiv p^2, q^2, r^2$  respectively, and let  $N \equiv 1$ . Then  $C, D \equiv \dfrac{1}{q^2}, \dfrac{1}{r^2}$ . Elementary calculations give  $X \equiv -(pq + pr + qr), Y \equiv - \left( \dfrac{pq + pr + 1}{qr} \right)$ . To show  $\angle XAY = \angle XNY$  we require
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{p^2 + pq + pr + qr}{p^2 + \dfrac{pq + pr + 1}{qr}} \cdot \dfrac{1 + \dfrac{pq + pr + 1}{qr}}{1 + pq + qr + rp} &= \dfrac{qr(p^2 + pq + pr + qr)}{p^2qr + pq + pr + 1} \cdot \dfrac{1 + pq + pr + qr}{qr(1 + pq + pr + qr)} 
&= \dfrac{p^2 + pq + pr + qr}{p^2qr + pq + pr + 1} 
&= \dfrac{(p + q)(p + r)}{(pq + 1)(pr + 1)} \in \mathbb{R}. 
\end{align*}
Clearly this equals its conjugate, then  $N \in (AXY)$  as wanted. We are done.

