<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $N=2025$ , and let  $x_1<x_2<\cdots<x_N$  be the elements of  $A$ . The first thing we're going to do is write  $P(A)$  and  $Q(A)$  as linear combinations of the  $x_k$ . Throughout this proof, we use the convention that  $\binom{n}r = 0$  unless  $0\le r\le n$ .

For  $P(A)$ , we count the number of times  $x_k$  shows up as a term in this sum -- that is, we're counting the subsets of  $A$  that have  $x_k$  as their median. This is just  $$ \sum_{\ell\ge 0}\binom{k-1}{\ell}\binom{N-k}{\ell} = \binom{N-1}{k-1} $$  by Vandermonde, so  $$ P(A) = \sum_{k=1}^N\binom{N-1}{k-1}x_k. $$  Similarly for  $Q(A)$ , let  $j < k$ , and we want to count the number of times  $x_j,x_k$  are the middle two elements of a subset  $T$  of  $A$ . This is  $$ \sum_{\ell\ge 0}\binom{j-1}{\ell}\binom{N-k}{\ell} = \binom{N-k+j-1}{j-1}, $$  by Vandermonde again, and so  $$ Q(A) = \sum_{1\le j<k\le N}\binom{N-k+j-1}{j-1}\frac{x_j+x_k}2. $$  We can collapse this double sum to a single sum using the hockey stick identity. We write
\begin{align*}
Q(A) &= \frac12\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}x_j\sum_{k=j+1}^N\binom{N-k+j-1}{j-1} + \frac12\sum_{k=2}^Nx_k\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\binom{N-k+j-1}{j-1} 
&= \frac12\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}\binom{N-1}jx_j + \frac12\sum_{k=2}^N\binom{N-1}{k-2}x_k 
&= \frac12\sum_{k=1}^N\left[\binom{N-1}k + \binom{N-1}{k-2}\right]x_k,
\end{align*}
and then  $$ P(A) - Q(A) = \sum_{k=1}^N\left[-\frac12\binom{N-1}k + \binom{N-1}{k-1} - \frac12\binom{N-1}{k-2}\right]x_k. $$  Now to account for the fact that  $x_1 < x_2 < \cdots < x_N$ , we let  $a_k$  denote their consecutive differences; that is,  $a_1 = x_1$  and  $a_k = x_k - x_{k-1}$  for  $2\le k\le N$ . So then  $a_k > 0$  for each  $k$ , and  $$ x_k = \sum_{j=1}^ka_j. $$  Then we rearrange and telescope the double sum to get
\begin{align*}
P(A) - Q(A) &= \sum_{k=1}^N\sum_{j=1}^k\left[-\frac12\binom{N-1}k + \binom{N-1}{k-1} - \frac12\binom{N-1}{k-2}\right]a_j 
&= \sum_{j=1}^Na_j\sum_{k=j}^N\left[-\frac12\binom{N-1}k + \binom{N-1}{k-1} - \frac12\binom{N-1}{k-2}\right] 
&= \frac12\sum_{j=1}^N\left[\binom{N-1}{j-1} - \binom{N-1}{j-2} + 1\right]a_j.
\end{align*}
Meanwhile,  $$ \max(A) = x_N = \sum_{j=1}^Na_j. $$  Now for each  $1\le j\le N$ , let  $$ c_j = \frac12\left[\binom{N-1}{j-1} - \binom{N-1}{j-2} + 1\right] $$  be the coefficient of  $a_j$  in  $P(A) - Q(A)$ .

If for any  $j$ ,  $C < c_j$ , then we can make  $P(A) - Q(A) > C\cdot \max(A)$  by taking  $a_j$  to be very large and all other  $a_k$  for  $k\neq j$  to be arbitrarily close to zero. However, if  $C\ge c_j$  for all  $j$ , then  $C\cdot \max(A) \ge P(A) - Q(A)$  term-by-term. So the answer we seek is  $$ C = \max_{1\le j\le N}c_j = \frac12 + \frac12\max_{1\le j\le N}\left[\binom{N-1}{j-1} - \binom{N-1}{j-2}\right]. $$  Now we write  $$ \binom{N-1}{j-1} - \binom{N-1}{j-2} = \binom{N-1}{j-1} - \frac{j-1}{N-j+1}\binom{N-1}{j-1} = \frac{N-2j+2}{N-j+1}\binom{N-1}{j-1} $$  and  $$ \binom{N-1}j - \binom{N-1}{j-1} = \frac{N-j}j\binom{N-1}{j-1} - \binom{N-1}{j-1} = \frac{N-2j}j\binom{N-1}{j-1}. $$  Subtracting these gives  $$ \left[\binom{N-1}j - \binom{N-1}{j-1}\right] - \left[\binom{N-1}{j-1} - \binom{N-1}{j-2}\right] = \left[\frac{N-2j}j - \frac{N-2j+2}{N-j+1}\right]\binom{N-1}{j-1}. $$  If this is positive, then  $j+1$  gives a larger difference than  $j$ . Combining denominators gives a numerator of  $$ (N-2j)(N-j+1) - j(N-2j+2) = 4j^2 - 4j(N+1) + N(N+1) $$  This quadratic has roots  $$ j = \frac{(N+1) \pm \sqrt{N+1}}2 $$  So as long as  $j$  is less than the smaller of these roots, our difference is larger if we use  $j+1$  instead. (Of course  $j$  is not larger than the larger of these roots, or else the difference is negative.) So the optimal value of  $j$  is  $$ j = \left\lceil\frac{(N+1)-\sqrt{N+1}}2\right\rceil. $$  and so the least possible value of  $C$  is  $$ C = \frac{N-2j+2}{2(N-j+1)}\binom{N-1}{j-1} + \frac12 $$  for this  $j$ . When  $N = 2025$ , we have  $$ j = \left\lceil\frac{2026-\sqrt{2026}}2\right\rceil = 991 $$  and so  $$ C = \frac{45}{2\cdot 1035}\binom{2024}{990} = \frac1{46}\binom{2024}{990} + \frac12. $$</details>

