First, observe that we only need the  $x^n$  coefficient of  $P(x):=p_0+p_1x+\cdots$  to not be divisible by  $p$ , since we can take  $c \in \mathbb{Z}$  such that  $cp_n \equiv 1 \pmod{p^k}$  and then take all coefficients modulo  $p^k$ . Now, observe that it is equivalent to maximize  $k$  such that  $p^k$  divides  $P(x)$  when  $p \mid x$ ; the answer in this scenario is clearly at least the answer to the original, but simultaneously if  $p^k \mid Q(x)$  for all  $x \mid p$  then  $(x-1)^k(x-2)^k\ldots (x-(p-1))^kQ(x)$  is divisible by  $p^k$  for all integers  $x$ . Finally, we may induct on  $n$  (with  $p$  fixed) and assume that all coefficients of  $P(x)$  with degree less than  $n$  are divisible by  $p$ , else by hypothesis the maximum power of  $p$  always dividing  $P(x)$  is less than  $n+\nu_p(n!)$  by hypothesis. Therefore, for any  $Q \in \mathbb{Z}[x]$  with  $p \nmid Q(0)$  we have  $Q(x)P(x)$  satisfies the conditions (with the first condition relaxed to  $p_n$  not being divisible by  $p$ ) if  $P(x)$  does.

Now, consider any polynomial  $P$  with WLOG  $x^n$  coefficient  $1$ . Pick some  $m>k$ ; we aim to construct  $Q(x)$  of the form  $q_mx^m+\cdots+q_1x+1$  (with all  $q_i \in \mathbb{Z}$ ) such that the  $x^{n+1}$  through  $x^m$  coefficients of  $Q(x)P(x)$  are  $0 \pmod{p^k}$ . Set  $q_m$  through  $q_{m-n+1}$  arbitrarily, and now we aim to find a solution  $q_1,\ldots,q_m$  to the system of equations  $$ \begin{array}{l l l l l l l}
q_1p_n+&q_2p_{n-1}+&\cdots+&q_{n+1}p_0+&0+&\cdots&= -p_{n+1}
q_1p_{n+1}+&q_2p_n+&\cdots+&q_{n+1}p_1+&q_{n+2}p_0+&\cdots&=-p_{n+2}
\vdots
& & & &\cdots+&q_{m-n}p_n&=-p_{n+m}-(q_{m-n+1}p_{n-1}+\cdots+q_mp_0).
\end{array} $$  By Cramer's the solution over  $\mathbb{Q}$  to this is of the form  $$ q_i=a_i\begin{vmatrix}p_n&p_{n-1}&\cdots&p_0&0&\cdotsp_{n-1}&p_n&\cdots&p_1&p_0&\cdots\vdots\end{vmatrix}^{-1}. $$  where  $a_i$  is an integer. Since  $p_{n-1},\ldots,p_0$  are all divisible by  $p$ , this matrix is lower diagonal modulo  $p$  and thus has determinant congruent to  $p_n^{m-n} \not \equiv 0$ . Therefore, there is also an integer solution to this system modulo  $p^k$ .

Now, work with  $Q(x)P(x)$  instead of  $P(x)$ . Since  $m>k$ ,  $p^k \mid x^m$ , so in fact it suffices to look at the remainder of  $Q(x)P(x)$  upon division by  $x^m$ , which has degree  $n$ ; call this  $f(x)$ , and WLOG (by multiplying) it has leading coefficient  $1$ . Now, consider the transform  $f(x) \mapsto f(x+p)-f(x)$ ; if  $p^k \mid f(x)$  for all  $p \mid x$  prior to the transform then the same is true after it. Applying this  $n$  times, we are left with the constant  $n!p^n$ , which yields the answer.  $\blacksquare$

