<details><summary>Solution (same idea as above two)</summary>Consider the sequence  $k^{k^a}$  where  $a\in\{1,2,3,...\}$ .
This has finitely many prime divisors (only primes dividing  $k$  divide the terms of this sequence). Hence, by Kobayashi, the sequence  $k^{k^a}-1$  has infinitely many prime divisors.

We will show that all primes dividing some term of this sequence always work. 
Choose such a prime  $p$  which divides  $k^{k^{a'}}-1$  for some  $a'$ . 
Observe that   $$ a_{n}=a_{n-1}+k^{a_{n-1}}=a_{n-2}+k^{a_{n-2}}+k^{a_{n-1}}=...=a_1+k^{a_1}+k^{a_2}+...+k^{a_{n-1}} $$  Hence,   $$ a_{n+1}-a_n=k^{a_n}=k^{a_1+k^{a_1}+k^{a_2}+...+k^{a_{n-1}}} $$  Let  $m$  be the largest index such that  $a_m<a'$  and define  $M=k^{a_1+k^{a_1}+k^{a_2}+...+k^{a_m}}$ .
 It can be trivially observed that  $p$  does not divide  $M$ .

Note that since  $p\mid k^{k^{a'}}-1$ ,  $p$  also divides  $k^{k^{b}}-1$  where  $b\ge a'$ .
Hence, choosing  $n\ge m$ , we get \begin{align*}
a_{n+1} - a_n 
&= k^{a_1 + k^{a_1} + k^{a_2} + \dots + k^{a_n}} 
&\equiv k^{a_1 + k^{a_1} + k^{a_2} + \dots + k^{a_m}} \cdot k^{k^{a_{m+1}}} \cdot k^{k^{a_{m+2}}} \dots  k^{k^{a_n}} 
&\equiv M \cdot 1 \cdot 1  \dots  1 \equiv M \pmod{p}
\end{align*}
Since  $a_{m+1}-a_m=M$ , it can be easily seen that   $$ a_{m+t}\equiv a_{m+t-1}+M\equiv ...\equiv a_m+M\cdot t(\text{mod}\,\,p) $$  Since  $M\cdot t$  covers all the residue classes modulo  $p$  and  $a_m$  is a constant, we conclude that for all naturals  $r$ ,  $\exists$    $t_r\in \mathbb{N}$  such that  $r\equiv a_m+t_r\cdot M\equiv a_{m+t_r}(\text{mod}\,\,p)$ . We're through :)</details>

