Divide the board into four quadrants as shown in the diagram. The upper-right and bottom-right quadrants ensure that  $3$  and  $4$  must be chosen from the same quadrant. This implies the same to be true for  $1$  and  $2$ , which we can easily check to be impossible.  $\square$ -----
[color = blue]**- Proof for  $n = 2, 3$ .**[/color]
Let  $(i, j)$  denote the cell in the  $i^{\text{th}}$  row and  $j^{\text{th}}$  column. The idea is to iteratively select numbers from each row, starting from row  $1$  in such a way that at each step, the numbers in the next row that lie in unchosen columns are exactly the unchosen numbers. The last pair of rows prevents the trivial approach, so we adjust by separately considering the first few rows.

Suppose rows  $n - 1$  and  $n$  differ in columns  $i$  and  $j$ . Let  $k$  be any other column. We take three cases.


- If rows  $1$  and  $2$  agree in at least one of the columns  $i$  or  $j$ , then we choose that column, say  $m$ , and add the cell  $(1, m)$  to the set.
- If rows  $1$  and  $2$  differ in  $i$  and  $j$ , but rows  $2$  and  $3$  agree on columns  $i$  or  $j$ , we again choose that column, say  $m$ , and add cells  $(1, k)$  and  $(2, m)$ .
- If rows  $(1, 2)$  and  $(2, 3)$  both differ in  $i$  and  $j$ , we add cells  $(1, i)$ ,  $(2, k)$  and  $(3, j)$  to the set.
[/list]
We continue from the highest row without a chosen cell and move downwards until row  $n - 1$ . Suppose we are at row  $i$ .
[list]
- If rows  $i$  and  $i + 1$  differ in exactly two unchosen columns, then  $i \neq n - 1$ , and we may choose any cell not in one of these two columns.
- If rows  $i$  and  $i + 1$  differ in one chosen column and one unchosen column, select the cell in the unchosen column.
- If rows  $i$  and  $i + 1$  differ in two chosen columns, we pick any unchosen cell.

Finally, choose the last remaining cell in row  $n$ . This gives us the desired set.  $\blacksquare$

