The answer is all powers of  $2$ . We first present an inductive construction:**Construction:** The base case of  $n = 4$  is trivial, so skip right to the inductive step. To create the  $2^n \times 2^n$  board, take the  $2^{n-1} \times 2^{n-1}$  board and place it in the top-left. Now, in the top-right, shift all elements of the  $2^{n-1} \times 2^{n-1}$  board up by  $2^{2n-2}$  and place it here. Repeat this for the bottom-right and left, and we have a valid labeling. 

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim:**</span> The sum of the labels of a  $2^k \times 2^k$  sub-board must be equivalent to  $2^{k-1} \pmod{2^k}$ *Proof.* We proceed with induction. For the  $k = 2$  case, split into  $2 \times 2$  sub-boards and note that each of these sub-boards has an odd sum, implying the total sum is even, which implies the result. Now, assume the result is true for  $2^n \times 2^n$  boards. Split a  $2^{n+1} \times 2^{n+1}$  board into four  $2^n \times 2^n$  boards. As a result, the total sum of the  $2^{n+1} \times 2^{n+1}$  board is divisible by  $2^{n}$ , which implies that it must be  $2^{n} \pmod{2^{n+1}}$ .  $\square$ To finish, take an  $n \times n$  board where  $n$  is not a power of  $2$ , and take  $d = 2^{\nu_2(n)}$ . There are an odd number  $d \times d$  sub-boards, so we have \begin{align*} \sum \text{label} \equiv (\tfrac{n}{d})^2 \cdot \sum_{d \times d} \text{label} \equiv (\tfrac{n}{d})^2 \cdot 2^{\nu_2(n) - 1} \equiv 2^{\nu_2(n) - 1}  \pmod{d} \end{align*}
However, the sum of all labels is also equal to  $\tfrac{(n^2)(n^2-1)}{2}$  which is clearly a multiple of  $d$ , contradiction.

