We claim the answer is  $\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$ .

We first show that  $\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$  exchanges are necessary. Consider an arrangement where each knight is diametrically opposite to her partner. We will define the *distance* between two knights to be the length of the shorter arc between them, scaled so that the distance between adjacent knights is  $1$ . Then the distance for every partner pair starts at  $n$ . Now for every partner pair, add ( $1$  if they have already shaken hands) or (their distance if they have not already shaken hands), and call this sum the *gravity* of an arrangement after some exchanges have been performed. Then then gravity of the initial arrangement is  $n^2$ , and will be  $n$  after every partner pair has shaken hands. However, we see that every exchange can decrease the contribution of the at most  $2$  partner pairs involved to the gravity each by at most  $1$ , and so decreases the gravity by at most  $2$ . Then at least  $\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$  exchanges are needed.

We now show that  $\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$  exchanges are sufficient. We first strengthen the setup: in the starting arrangement, connect partner pairs by a *spaghetto* that wraps around the table either a counterclockwise or clockwise arc, whose length is the length of that arc. Then when the knights in a pair move toward each other, the spaghetto decreases in length by  $1$ , and when they move away from each other, the spaghetto increases in length by  $1$ . In the initial configuration, of the two possible spaghetti a pair of knights could be connected by, we choose the shorter one, and if there is a tie, we may pick either spaghetto, both of which will have length  $n$ .
[asy]
size(5cm);

real r = 0.5;
real knightradius = 0.1;
real sgap = 0.075;

void knight(picture p, int n, int i, pen c){
	fill(p, circle((1 + r) * dir(360 * i / n), knightradius), c);
}
void table(picture p){
	draw(p, circle((0, 0), 1));
}
void spaghetto(picture p, int n, int s, int e, int slayer, pen c){
	draw(p, arc((0, 0), 1 + sgap * slayer, 360 * s / n, 360 * e / n, direction=CCW), c);

}

int n = 6;
real s = 100;

picture t1;
size(t1, s);
table(t1);
knight(t1, n, 0, red);
