We now claim that given any starting arrangement, we can perform at most  $\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$  exchanges to make all spaghetti have length  $1$ . Now for each of the  $\binom{n}{2}$  pairs of spaghetti, add ( $0$  if they do not intersect), ( $1$  if they intersect but one is not contained within the other), or ( $2$  if one is contained within the other), and call this sum the *entanglement* of an arrangement. Note that the entanglement is  $0$  if and only if every spaghetto has length  $1$ .

We first show that the entanglement of any arrangement is at most  $\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$ . We prove this by induction on  $n$ . The base case of  $n=1$  is immediate. Now suppose this is true for all arrangements with  $n-1$  pairs, and now consider one with  $n$  pairs, for some  $n\geq 2$ . Consider the partner pair  $AB$  with the longest spaghetto. Ignoring  $AB$ , the contribution to the entanglement from the rest of the spaghetti is at most  $\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}$  by the induction hypothesis. Now add back  $AB$ . Note that  $AB$  is not contained within another spaghetto. Consider the pairs of spaghetti formed by  $AB$  and another pair  $XY$ ; if such a pair contributes  $1$  to the entanglement, then one of  $X,Y$  is inside  $AB$ 's spaghetto, and if it contributes  $2$  to the entanglement, then both  $X,Y$  are inside  $AB$ 's spaghetto. But since there are at most  $n-1$  knights inside  $AB$ 's spaghetto, the total contribution of these pairs is at most  $n-1$ , and so the total entanglement is at most \[\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2}+(n-1)=\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\] This completes the induction.

We now show that there is always an exchange that decreases the entanglement if it is nonzero. Number the knights  $0,1,2\dots,n-1$  in counterclockwise order, with indices taken modulo  $n$ . Let  $k>1$  be the largest length of a spaghetto, and pick a spaghetto  $t\to t+k$  (where this is a counterclockwise arc) of length  $k$ . The only way that swapping  $t$  and  $t+1$  would not decrease the entanglement is if  $(t+1)$ 's spaghetto contained  $t+k$ , which is only possible if it is the spaghetto  $(t+1)\to (t+k+1)$ . Repeating this argument on  $t+1$  and so on, if there is no such exchange that decreases the entanglement, then \[t\to t+k,t+1\to t+k+1,t+2\to t+k+2,\dots\] are all spaghetti. But then  $t\to t+k$  and  $t+k\to t+2k$  are both spaghetti, which is a contradiction. Then there must be some such exchange that decreases the entanglement.

Now since the original entanglement is at most  $\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$  and while it is nonzero we can perform an exchange decreasing it, we must be able to get to an entanglement of  $0$  in at most  $\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$  exchanges. At this point, all partner pairs will be adjacent, which is sufficient.

We claim the answer is  $\frac{n(n-1)}{2}$ . This is achieved when student  $i$  places in  $i$ th place in every race.

We now show this is indeed the maximum. Let  $(a_i,b_i)$  be a rating for the  $i$ th student. Let  $S_k$  be the set of students  $i$  with  $b_i=k$ , and let  $x_k=\sum_{i\in S_k}a_i$ . We have that since there are at most  $nk$  spots in the top  $k$  places over all races, \[x_1+x_2+\dots+x_k\leq nk\] Also, since each student has  $a_i\leq n$ , \[|S_k|\geq\frac{x_k}{n}\] We now have
\begin{align*}
    &\sum_{i=1}^n(a_i-b_i)
    =&\sum_{k=1}^n (x_k-k|S_k|)
    \leq&\sum_{k=1}^n \left(x_k-\frac{kx_k}{n}\right)
    =&\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\sum_{\ell=1}^k\frac{x_\ell}{n}
    \leq&\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}k
    =&\frac{n(n-1)}{2}
\end{align*}
as desired.

solution_md:
A little disappointed. If  $\mathcal S = \{2\}$ , the result is clear. Observe that the series  $\sum \frac 1{b_n}$  is bounded above, so throughout, assume that  $n$  is sufficiently large such that the ceiling on the right side is maximum possible.

We split into two cases now.**<span style="color:blue">First Case:**</span> Suppose that  $\mathcal S = \{2, 3\}$ . Fix some large  $n$  (described before), and let  $k = \lfloor \log_3 n \rfloor$ ; for each  $0 \leq r \leq k$ , set  $m_r$  to be the largest nonnegative integer such that  $3^r \cdot m_r \leq b_n$ . We may write the sum on the right as \[\sum_{i=1}^n \frac 1{b_i} = \sum_{r=0}^k \sum_{i=0}^{m_r} \frac 1{3^r \cdot 2^i} = \sum_{r=0}^k \left(\frac 2{3^r} - \frac 1{3^r \cdot 2^{m_r}}\right).\] In particular, it then follows that 
\[\frac 1{3^k} + \sum_{r=0}^k \frac 1{3^r \cdot 2^{m_r}} + \sum_{i=1}^n \frac 1{b_i}
= \frac 1{3^k} + \sum_{r=0}^k \frac 2{3^r} = 3.\]
The right hand side equals the ceiling of  $\sum \frac 1{b_n}$ , and the right hand side consists of a sum of unit fractions with denominators at most  $b_n$ , as needed.**<span style="color:blue">Second Case:**</span> Assume that  $\mathcal S$  is not  $\{2\}$  or  $\{2, 3\}$ .**<span style="color:#f00">Claim:**</span> The infinite sum  $\sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac 1{b_i}$  is a non-integer rational.

*Proof:* The infinite sum equals \[\sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac 1{b_i} = \prod_{p \in \mathcal S} \left(1+\frac 1{p-1}\right).\] If  $\mathcal S$  is not equal to  $\{2, p\}$  for some odd prime  $p$ , the infinite sum has a negative  $2$ -adic valuation, so it cannot be an integer. If  $\mathcal S$  is  $\{2, p\}$  for some  $p \geq 3$ , the product  $\tfrac{2p}{p-1}$  also cannot be an integer.  $\blacksquare$ So now let  $\sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac 1{b_i} = \frac cd$  in lowest terms. Let  $p_1, \dots, p_k$  be the primes in  $\mathcal S$ . Let  $n$  be sufficiently large, and define  $M_r$  as the maximum valuation of  $p_r$  among the existing denominators. In addition, let  $m_1, \dots, m_r$  be positive integers such that  $p_1^{m_1} \cdots p_r^{m_r} < b_n$ . Consider ``partial sums"  $S_r$  given by  $S_0 = \sum_{i=1}^n \frac 1{b_i}$ , and 
\[S_r = S_{r-1} + \frac{a_{M_rr}}{p_r^{M_r}} + \frac{a_{(M_r-1)r}}{p_r^{M_r-1}} + \cdots + \frac{a_{(m_r+1)r}}{p_r^{m_r + 1}}.\] We can always pick  $a_{ir}$  for each  $m_r + 1 \leq i \leq M_r$  between  $0$  and  $p_r$  such that the partial sum \[S_{ir} = S_{r-1} + \frac{a_{M_rr}}{p_r^{M_r}} + \cdots + \frac{a_{ir}}{p_r^i}\] has  $p$ -adic valuation at least  $-i+1$  for each  $i$  (by taking  $a_{ir}$  negative the ``remainder" of the existing partial sum modulo  $p^i$ ). In particular,  $S_r$  has  $p$ -adic valuation at least  $-m_r$ . Then, size-wise, we have
\begin{align*}
S_r &\leq S_{r-1} + \frac{p_r-1}{p_r^{M_r}} + \cdots + \frac{p_r-1}{p_r^{m_r+1}} 
&= S_{r-1} + \frac 1{p_r^{M_r-1}} - \frac 1{p_r^{M_r}} + \cdots + \frac 1{p_r^{m_r}} - \frac 1{p_r^{m_r+1}} 
&= S_{r-1} + \frac 1{p_r^{m_r}} - \frac 1{p_r^{M_r - 1}}.
\end{align*}
Thus \[S_k \leq \sum_{i=1}^n \frac 1{b_i} + \sum_{r=1}^k \left(\frac 1{p_r^{m_r}} - \frac 1{p_r^{M_r-1}}\right) \leq \sum_{i=1}^n \frac 1{b_i} + \frac 1d\] by taking  $n$  sufficiently large. Since  $\nu_{p_r}(S_k) \geq -m_r$  for every  $r$ , the denominator of  $S_k$  is a divisor of  $p_1^{m_1} \cdots p_r^{m_r}$ , which is some  $b_\ell < n$ . Observe that by definition,  $S_k$  is the sum of  $\sum_{i=1}^n \frac 1{b_i}$  with unit fractions of denominators some  $b_i$ , and by the previous observation, we may add some  $\frac L{b_\ell}$  to  $S_k$  to get the ceiling, as needed.**<span style="color:purple">Remark:**</span> The way the  $\{2, 3\}$  case collapses is really nice, and the rest of the proof seems really lame in comparison.

