This calculation/proof is part of a broader trend in heyawake puzzles called **penalty theory**.  It applies when you want to place a maximum (or almost-maximum) number of black squares in the grid, like in this puzzle (due to **coggy** on PuzzleSquareJP):
[asy]
size(200);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.6));
for(int i=0;i<=11;i=i+1)
{
draw((0,i)--(9,i));
}
for(int j=0;j<=9;j=j+1)
{
draw((j,0)--(j,12));
draw((0,0)--(0,12)--(9,12)--(9,0)--(0,0)^^(3,0)--(3,12)^^(3,1)--(9,1),linewidth(2.5));
label(" $11$ ",(0.5,11.5));
label(" $24$ ",(3.5,11.5));
}
[/asy]
The idea is to treat the grid of cells as a graph and track "penalties", i.e. configurations or squares that prevent you from *actually* reaching a theoretical maximum of black squares.  (For example, black squares along the edges are more prohibitive than ones in the interior of the grid.)  The ISL problem is asking for all values of  $n$  for which an  $n\times n$  square admits zero penalties.

There are quite a few writeups about penalty theory in general; see [here](https://tck.mn/blog/penalty-theory/), [here](https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c2532359h2747309_heyawake_part_2__penalty_theory), and [here](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1WW2WKuCNrakIXDomgt5onrL38H1FAWU0onLa-3KXF0M/edit?tab=t.0#heading=h.czfcddt6p42n).  (Unfortunately, I don't know the theory myself, oops!)

