Now consider again the grid  $A$ . This is divided into  $2$  sets: the vertices of the squares and the center of the squares. One of this set is in  $S$  because we cannot have elements of  $A\setminus S$  in both the parts. If every center is in  $S$  we have a cycle because we can follow the boundary of the grid. 
