for (int i=0;i<4;++i) {
for (int j=0;j<4;++j) {
dot((i,-j),blue);
}
}
for (int i=0;i<3;++i) {
for (int j=0;j<3;++j) {
dot((i+0.5,-j-0.5));
draw((i+0.5,-j-0.5)--(i,-j));
draw((i+0.5,-j-0.5)--(i+1,-j));
draw((i+0.5,-j-0.5)--(i,-j-1));
draw((i+0.5,-j-0.5)--(i+1,-j-1));
}
}

[/asy]
The points blue in picture are in  $S$  so we have to choose which black points are in  $S$ .
If we look at which condition the problem ask for this point we find the same problem but with  $\dfrac{n}{2}$  points because the blue points became the cells and the black points became the  $2\times 2$  squares.
So if  $n\ne 1$  is a solution of the problem then  $\dfrac{n}{2}$  is too. 
This conclude the proof because of induction.
This problem is easier than it looks and two hours are more than enough to solve it.

