The answer is  $\boxed{n\ne1,2}$ . We see that  $n=1,2$  do not work by letting  $P(x)=1$ . Now, we will show that all  $n\ne1,2$  satisfy the problem condition.

By replacing  $P$  with  $-P$ , it suffices that for all integer polynomials  $P$  we have that there exists a quadratic integer polynomial  $Q$  with  $$ Q(k)(Q(k)-P(k))\not\equiv0\pmod{n} $$  for all integers  $k$ . Let  $p$  be a prime divisor of  $n$ , or  $4$  if  $n$  is a multiple of  $4$ . We will show that we can pick  $Q(x)$  so that we always have  $Q(k)(Q(k)-P(k))\not\equiv0\pmod{p}$ , which suffices. First, if  $p=3,4$ , then we can <details><summary>manually check</summary>For  $p=3$ , note that the  $8$  quadratics  $$ 3x^2+1,3x^2+2,x^2+1,(x-1)^2+1,(x-2)^2+1,-x^2-1,-(x-1)^2-1,-(x-2)^2-1 $$  cover every possible function  $f$  on the residues  $\pmod{3}$  with  $f(k)\not\equiv0\pmod{3}$  for all  $k$ . Then there exists such an  $f$  with  $f(k)\not\equiv{}P(k)\pmod{3}$  for all  $k$ , so we are done.

For  $p=4$ , we will take split into cases based on how many residues  $k\pmod{4}$  satisfy  $P(k)\equiv3\pmod{4}$ .

If  $P(x)$  takes the value  $3\pmod{4}$  no times then we can take  $Q(x)\equiv3\pmod{4}$ .

If  $P(x)$  takes the value  $3\pmod{4}$  once, then without loss of generality let  $P(0)\equiv3\pmod{4}$ . Then if  $P(2)\equiv1\pmod{4}$  we can take  $Q(x)\equiv{}x^2+2\pmod{4}$  and otherwise we can take  $Q(x)\equiv{}2x^2+1\pmod{4}$ .

If  $P(x)$  takes the value  $3\pmod{4}$  twice, then without loss of generality let  $P(0)\equiv3\pmod{4}$ . Then if  $P(1)\equiv3\pmod{4}$  we can take  $Q(x)\equiv{}x^2-x+1\pmod{4}$ , if  $P(2)\equiv3\pmod{4}$  we can take  $Q(x)\equiv{}x^2+2\pmod{4}$ , and if  $P(3)\equiv3\pmod{4}$  we can take  $Q(x)\equiv{}x^2+x+1\pmod{4}$ .

If  $P(x)$  takes the value  $3\pmod{4}$  three times, then without loss of generality let  $P(0)\not\equiv3\pmod{4}$ . If  $P(0)\equiv1\pmod{4}$  then we can take  $Q(x)\equiv{}2-x^2\pmod{4}$ , and otherwise we can take  $Q(x)\equiv{}x^2+1\pmod{4}$ .

If  $P(x)$  takes the value of  $3\pmod{4}$  four times, then we can take  $Q(x)\equiv1\pmod{4}$ .

Therefore, we are done.</details> that there exists  $Q(x)$ .

Now, if  $p\ge5$  is prime, we will consider  $$ Q(x)\equiv{}a\left((x-c)^2-b\right)\pmod{p} $$  for  $a\not\equiv0\pmod{p}$  and  $b$  a quadratic nonresidue  $\pmod{p}$ . Note that  $Q(k)\not\equiv0\pmod{p}$ . We claim that we can choose one such  $Q(x)$  such that  $Q(k)\not\equiv{}P(k)\pmod{p}$  for all integers  $k$ , which suffices.

Assume otherwise. Then assume  $P(k)\not\equiv0\pmod{p}$  for all  $k$ , as otherwise we can ignore that choice of  $k$ . We then fix any particular  $b$  and  $c$ , and note that for all  $p-1$  choices of  $a$  we must have  $Q(k)\equiv{}P(k)\pmod{p}$  for some  $k$ . As each  $k$  corresponds to exactly one choice of  $a$  so that  $Q(k)\equiv{}P(k)\pmod{p}$ , but all choices of  $a$  must correspond to at least one  $k$ , we see that exactly one choice of  $a$  has  $$ Q(k)\equiv{}P(k)\pmod{p} $$  for two  $k$ . We now note that over all  $b$  and  $c$  we must get exactly  $\tfrac{p(p-1)}{2}$  possible  $Q$  for which  $Q(k)\equiv{}P(k)\pmod{p}$  for two  $k$ . However, we can see that for every  $2$  distinct  $j,k\pmod{p}$  we have that there exists at least  $1$  choice of  $Q$  so that  $Q(j)\equiv{}P(j)\pmod{p}$  and  $Q(k)\equiv{}P(k)\pmod{p}$  by <details><summary>just solving for a, b, and c</summary>We get that  $a\left((j-c)^2-b\right)\equiv{}P(j)\pmod{p}$  and  $a\left((k-c)^2-b\right)\equiv{}P(k)\pmod{p}$ . For there to exist an  $a$  for which this is true, it suffices that  $\tfrac{(j-c)^2-b}{(k-c)^2-b}\equiv\tfrac{P(j)}{P(k)}\pmod{p}$ , or  $P(k)\left((j-c)^2-b\right)-P(j)\left((k-c)^2-b\right)\equiv0\pmod{p}$ . If  $P(j)\equiv{}P(k)\pmod{p}$  then this is linear in  $b$  and  $c$  and so we can find a choice of  $b$  and  $c$  which satisfies the equation. Otherwise, we note that this equation is of the form  $$ A(c-B)^2+C-Db\equiv0\pmod{p}. $$  We can <details><summary>verify</summary>Expanding gives  $(P(k)-P(j))c^2-2(jP(k)-kP(j))c+j^2P(k)-k^2P(j)-(P(k)-P(j))b\equiv0\pmod{p}$ , or  $$ (P(k)-P(j))\left(c-\frac{jP(k)-kP(j)}{P(k)-P(j)}\right)^2-\frac{(jP(k)-kP(j))^2-(P(k)-P(j))\left(j^2P(k)-k^2P(j)\right)}{P(k)-P(j)}-(P(k)-P(j))b\equiv0\pmod{p}. $$  Now we note that  $$ (jP(k)-kP(j))^2-(P(k)-P(j))\left(j^2P(k)-k^2P(j)\right)\equiv(j-k)^2P(j)P(k)\not\equiv0\pmod{p} $$  and so  $C\not\equiv0\pmod{p}$ . Also  $D\equiv{}P(k)-P(j)\not\equiv0\pmod{p}$ .</details> that  $C\not\equiv0\pmod{p}$ . Now, it suffices to pick  $b$  so that  $\tfrac{Db-C}{A}$  is not a quadratic residue, as then we can pick an appropriate  $c$  so that  $(c-B)^2\equiv\tfrac{Db-C}{A}\pmod{p}$ . Since  $\tfrac{D}{A}b-\tfrac{C}{A}$  is linear with every coefficient nonzero, it is well known that this is possible when  $p\ge5$ , but here is a <details><summary>proof</summary>Let  $g$  be any quadratic nonresidue  $\pmod{p}$ , so that  $b$  is of the form  $gk^2$  for  $x\not\equiv0\pmod{p}$ . Let  $E=\tfrac{D}{A}\cdot{}g$  and  $F=-\tfrac{C}{A}$ , so we want  $Ex^2+F$  to be a quadratic residue. We will evaluate  $$ \sum^{p-1}_{x=1}\left(\frac{Ex^2+F}{p}\right)\pmod{p} $$  and show that it is not  $-(p-1)$ , which suffices. This is because
\begin{align*}
\sum^{p-1}_{x=0}\left(\frac{Ex^2+F}{p}\right)&\equiv\sum^{p-1}_{x=0}\left(Ex^2+F\right)^{\frac{p-1}{2}}
&\equiv\sum^{p-1}_{x=0}\sum^{\frac{p-1}{2}}_{k=0}\binom{\frac{p-1}{2}}{k}E^{\frac{p-1}{2}-k}F^kx^{p-1-2k}
&\equiv\sum^{\frac{p-1}{2}}_{k=0}\binom{\frac{p-1}{2}}{k}E^{\frac{p-1}{2}-k}F^k\sum^{p-1}_{x=0}x^{p-1-2k}
&\equiv{}E^{\frac{p-1}{2}}\sum^{p-1}_{x=0}x^{p-1}
&\equiv-E^{\frac{p-1}{2}}\pmod{p},
\end{align*}
meaning that  $$ \sum^{p-1}_{x=1}\left(\frac{Ex^2+F}{p}\right)\equiv-E^{\frac{p-1}{2}}-\left(\frac{F}{p}\right)\equiv-2,0,2\pmod{p}, $$  but  $-(p-1)\equiv1\pmod{p}$ .</details>.</details>. Also, there is more than  $1$  choice for  $Q$  when  $P(j)\equiv{}P(k)\pmod{p}$  by taking  $c\equiv\tfrac{P(j)+P(k)}{2}\pmod{p}$  and  $b$  any quadratic nonresidue, then choosing the appropriate  $a\pmod{p}$ . Then we must get strictly more than  $\tfrac{p(p-1)}{2}$  choices of  $Q$  because we have at least  $1$  pair  $(j,k)$  with  $j\not\equiv{}k\pmod{p}$  and  $P(j)\equiv{}P(k)\pmod{p}$  by pigeonhole since  $P(k)\not\equiv0\pmod{p}$  for all  $k$ , a contradiction, so we are done.

stem_md:
Let  $p \ne q$  be coprime positive integers. Determine all infinite sequences  $a_1$ ,  $a_2$ ,  $\dots$  of positive integers such that the following conditions hold for all  $n \ge 1$ :
\begin{align*}
    \max(a_n, a_{n+1}, \dots, a_{n+p}) - \min(a_n, a_{n+1}, \dots, a_{n+p}) &= p 
    \max(a_n, a_{n+1}, \dots, a_{n+q}) - \min(a_n, a_{n+1}, \dots, a_{n+q}) &= q   
\end{align*}

solution_md:
Bary on  $\triangle MCE$  with  $M=(1,0,0), C=(0,1,0), E=(0,0,1)$     Denote  $a=|CE|, b=|ME|, c=|MC|$     Let  $X=EM\cap (MBC), \angle DEX=\angle DAB, \angle DXA=\angle DBA, D$  spirals segment  $EX$  to  $AB$     Thus,  $\angle EDX=\angle ADB$  so,  $Q=XD\cap CE$      $A=(k+2c:b:-c)$      $B=(k+2b:-b:c)$  For any real constant k, one check that this satisfies the fact that  $M$  is the midpoint of  $CE$      $(BMC):ayz+bxz+cxy=(x+y+z)(\frac{-ab}{k+b+c}z)$      $(AME):ayz+bxz+cxy=(x+y+z)(\frac{-ac}{k+b+c}y)$      $D=(BMC)\cap (AME)=(d:b:c)$      $abc+2dbc=(d+b+c)(\frac{-abc}{k+b+c})$      $\iff a+2d+\frac{(d+b+c)a}{(k+b+c)}=0$      $\iff d(2k+2b+2c+a)+a(2b+2c+k)=0$      $\implies \boxed{d=\frac{-a(2b+2c+k)}{a+2k+2b+2c}}$     Let  $L=(L:1:0)$      $\overline{LBD}\iff \begin{vmatrix}
    L & 1 & 0 
    -k-2b & b & -c 
    d & b & c 
\end{vmatrix}=0$  $\iff \begin{vmatrix}
    L & 1 & 0 
    -k-2b & b & -c 
    d-k-2b & 2b & 0 
\end{vmatrix}=0$ \begin{align*}
\implies 2b^2L&=d-k-2b
&=-(\frac{-2ab+2ac+ak+ak+2k^2+2bk+2ck+2ab+4kb+4b^2+4bc}{a+2k+2b+2c})
&=-2(\frac{k^2+(a+3b+c)k+2ab+2ac+2bc+b^2}{2k+a+2b+2c}
\end{align*} $\implies \boxed{L=-\frac{k^2+(a+3b+c)k+2ab+2ac+2bc+b^2}{b(2k+a+2b+2c)}}$ Now let  $x=(x:0:1)$ . 
Since  $x$  lies on  $(BMC)$ , $bx=(x+1)(\frac{-ab}{k+b+c})$  $\iff x+(x+1)(\frac{a}{k+b+c})=0$ Thus,  $\boxed{x=(-a:0:k+a+b+c)}$ Let  $Q=XD\cap CE=(0:1:q)$  $\overline{QXD}\iff \begin{vmatrix}
    0 & 1 & q 
    -a & 0 & k+a+b+c 
    -\frac{a(k+2b+2c)}{a+2k+2b+2c} & b & c 
\end{vmatrix}=0$  $\iff -abq=-ac+a\frac{(k+a+b+c)(k+2b+2c)}{a+2k+2b+2c}$  $\iff -bq(a+2k+2b+2c)=(k+a+b+c)(k+2b+2c)-(a+2k+2b+2c)c=k^2+(a+3b+c)K+2ab+2bc+ac+2b^2$ Denote  $M=k^2+(a+3b+c)K+2ab+2bc+ac+2b^2$  $\boxed{Q=(0:-b(a+2k+2b+2c):M), L=(-M:(a+2k+2b+2c):b:0)}$ Let  $W'=LQ\cap MD=(w:b:c)$  $\overline{W'LQ}\iff \begin{vmatrix}
    w & b & c 
    0 & -b(a+2k+2b+2c) & M 
    -M & (a+2k+2b+2c)b & 0 
\end{vmatrix}=0$  $\iff \begin{vmatrix}
    w & 0 & c+\frac{M}{a+2k+2b+2c  } 
    0 & -b(a+2k+2b+2c) & M 
    -M& (a+2k+2b+2c)b & 0 
\end{vmatrix}=0$  $\iff -wM(a+2k+2b+2c)b+m(\frac{M}{a+2k+2b+2c}+c)b=0$  $\iff w=\frac{\frac{M}{a+2k+2b+2c}+c}{a+2k+2b+2c}=\frac{k^2+(a+3b+c)k+2ac+2c^2+2b^2+2ab+4bc}{(a+2k+2b+2c)^2}$ Which is symmetric in  $b$  and  $c$ , thus  $LQ$ ,  $MD$ ,  $KP$  concur as desired

