*proof*. Note that  $E'CA$  is isosceles triangle with  $E'A=CA$ . Similar for  $AC=AF'$ , so 
\[ \angle AE'C=90^\circ -\frac 12\angle E'AC==90^\circ -\frac 12\angle BDF'=\angle BF'D \]
thus  $B, C, E', F'$  is concyclic. By reim's, we see that  $AD\parallel E'F'\parallel EF$  as needed. **<span style="color:#f00">claim</span>**. Point  $O_1$ , which is the center of  $(B, C, E', F')$ , lies on  $(ABCD)$ . 
*proof*. Because 
\[ \angle BO_1C=180^\circ-\angle CAB=2\angle AE'C. \]

Now, we see that the bisectors of segments  $BC$ ,  $E'F'$  intersects on  $(ABCD)$ , namely  $O_1$ . We claim that this is equivalent to the problem statement. Indeed, if  $K$  is a point on the line passing  $O$  and perpendicular to  $AD$ , and  $E_1$ ,  $F_1$  are the reflection over  $K$ , then as  $K$  moves over the line, the bisector of  $E_1F_1$  is fixed. In particular, when  $K$  is the midpoint of  $AD$ , then obviously  $O_1$  is the antipode of  $M$  on  $(ABCD)$ , as needed.

We claim that the answer is  $\boxed{4}$ . As a construction, consider the mapuche sequence with  $a_1=1,a_2=1,a_3=2$  and  $a_4=2$ . The idea is that  $a_i$  for all  $i \ge 5$  cannot be an integer. We show this by claiming that these reals are all in the range  $(1,2)$ .

Let  $S_i = a_1+\dots + a_i$  and  $P_i = a_1\dots a_i$  for each  $i \ge 1$ . It is easy to see that
\[a_n = \frac{S_{n-1}}{P_{n-1}}\]
from the problem statement. An extremely straightforward calculation yields  $a_2=1$  ,  $a_3 = a_4 = \frac{a_1+1}{a_1}$ . Hence, it is easy to check that  $P_4+1<S_4<2P_4$  (and hence  $a_5$  is not an integer). We now resort to induction.

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim :**For all positive integers  $i \ge 4$  we have  $S_i >P_i+1$ . </span>

*Proof :*The base case being checked, we now deal with the inductive step. Note that assuming  $S_{n-1}>P_{n-1}+1$  we have
\begin{align*}
S_n &= S_{n-1}+ a_n 
&= S_{n-1} + \frac{S_{n-1}}{P_{n-1}}
&= \frac{S_{n-1}(P_{n-1}+1)}{P_{n-1}}
& > \frac{S_{n-1}P_{n-1}+P_{n-1}}{P_{n-1}}
&= S_{n-1}+1 
&= P_n +1
\end{align*}
as desired.

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim :**For all positive integers  $i \ge 4$  we have  $S_i<2P_i$ .</span>

*Proof :*The base case being checked, we deal with the inductive step. Assuming  $S_{n-1}<2P_{n-1}$  we have,
\begin{align*}
S_n &= S_{n-1}+a_n
&= S_{n-1} + \frac{S_{n-1}}{P_{n-1}}
&= \frac{S_{n-1}(P_{n-1}+1)}{P_{n-1}}
& < \frac{2S_{n-1}P_{n-1}}{P_{n-1}}
& = 2S_{n-1}
&= 2P_n
\end{align*}
with the inequality due to the obvious fact that  $P_{n-1}>1$  (it is easy to check that  $P_4>1$  and hence  $P_i>1$  for all successive  $i$  as  $a_i>1$  by the previous claim).

Hence, for all  $i \ge 4$  we have that
\[1<a_{i+1} = \frac{S_i}{P_i}<2\]
which implies that they are all non-integers, as desired.

stem_md:
Consider an \(n\times n\) board divided into \(n^2\) cells, with \(n\ge3\). Initially, one cell is chosen and \(n^2\) coins are placed on it. A move consists of choosing a cell that contains at least two coins and moving two of those coins to two cells that are symmetric with respect to the chosen cell and share at least one vertex with it. The four possible types of moves are shown below.
[asy]

unitsize(1cm);
defaultpen(linewidth(1.2)+fontsize(10pt));
pen gridpen = linewidth(1.2);
pen coinstroke = linewidth(1.2);
pen coinfill = rgb(1,0.8,0); // orange-like

void drawGrid(real xo){
  for (int i=0; i<=3; ++i){
    draw( (xo+i,0)--(xo+i,3), gridpen );
    draw( (xo, i)--(xo+3, i), gridpen );
  }
}

void board(int t, real xo, string labeltext){
  drawGrid(xo);
  pair C = (xo+1.5,1.5);
  filldraw(circle(C,0.08), black, black);

  pair P1, P2;
  if (t==1){ P1=(xo+1.5,2.5); P2=(xo+1.5,0.5); }         
  if (t==2){ P1=(xo+0.5,1.5); P2=(xo+2.5,1.5); }             
  if (t==3){ P1=(xo+0.5,0.5); P2=(xo+2.5,2.5); }         
  if (t==4){ P1=(xo+0.5,2.5); P2=(xo+2.5,0.5); }            

  filldraw(circle(P1,0.25), coinfill, coinstroke);
  filldraw(circle(P2,0.25), coinfill, coinstroke);

  draw(C--P1, EndArrow(size=8));
  draw(C--P2, EndArrow(size=8));

  label(labeltext, (xo+1.5,-0.45));
}

board(1,0,"Type 1");
board(2,4,"Type 2");
board(3,8,"Type 3");
board(4,12,"Type 4");
[/asy]
If after several moves there is exactly one coin on every cell of the board, prove that the number of Type 3 moves is equal to the number of Type 4 moves.

