\end{align*}
Similarly  $y = \tfrac{ac(1 + \omega)}{a + c}$  and  $z = \tfrac{ab(1 + \omega)}{a + b}$ . 

First we compute the circumcenter of a triangle with vertices  $\tfrac{a^2(b - c)}{(a + c)}$ ,  $\tfrac{b^2(a - c)}{(b + c)}$ ,  $0$ . This is simply,
\begin{align*}
-\tfrac{a^2b^2(a - c)(b - c)}{(a + c)(b + c)} \left[\tfrac{c - a}{ab(b + c)} - \tfrac{c - b}{ab(a + c)} \right] \div \left[\tfrac{a^2(b - c)(c - a)}{ab(a + c)(b + c)} -  \tfrac{b^2(a - c)(c - b)}{ab(a + c)(b + c)}\right] &= -\tfrac{ab(a - c)(b - c)(b^2 - a^2)}{(a + c)^2(b + c)^2} \div \tfrac{(c - b)(c - a)(b^2 - a^2)}{ab(a + c)(b + c)}
&= -\tfrac{a^2b^2}{(a + c)(b + c)}
\end{align*}

The circumcenter of  $\triangle ABC$  is  $abc(1 + \omega)$  times the circumcenter of the triangle with vertices  $\tfrac{1}{a(b + c)}$ ,  $\tfrac{1}{b(a + c)}$ , and  $\tfrac{1}{c(a + b)}$ . Shifting the third vertex to  $0$ , we find the vertices  $\tfrac{b(a - c)}{ac(a + b)(b + c)}$ ,  $\tfrac{a(b - c)}{bc(a + b)(a + c)}$ , which scaling up by  $abc(a + b)$  yields the vertices we found the circumcenter of earlier. Reversing all the operations, the circumcenter of  $\triangle ABC$  is,
\begin{align*}
abc(1 + \omega) \left[ -\tfrac{a^2b^2}{(a + c)(b + c)} \cdot \tfrac{1}{(a + b)abc} + \tfrac{1}{c(a + b)} \right] = \tfrac{abc(a + b + c)(1 + \omega)}{(a + b)(b + c)(c + a)}
\end{align*}
Note that  $H_1 = a + b + c$  and  $H_2 = \omega(a + b + c)$ . Hence it suffices to show  $(1 + \omega)(a + b + c)$  is a real multiple of the circumcenter of  $\triangle ABC$ . This is true if and only if,
\begin{align*}
\tfrac{abc}{(a + b)(b + c)(c + a)} \in \mathbb{R}
\end{align*}
which is easily checked to be true. Note that this implies for any choice of  $\triangle A_1B_1C_1$ , and rotation factor  $\omega$ , the claim holds; hence it must necessarily hold for all choices of  $\triangle ABC$ . Hence the claim is true, as desired. 

