<span style="color:#008000">**Solution 2 (amusing DDIT, mine)**</span>

First, we introduce the following lemmas.

<span style="color:#00f">**<u>Lemma 1</u>**</span>: Let \( \mathcal{E} \) be an ellipse and \( K \) an external point. Suppose two lines pass through \( K \) and are tangent to \( \mathcal{E} \), touching \( \mathcal{E} \) at \( X, Y \). Then the two foci of \( \mathcal{E} \) are isogonal conjugates with respect to \( \angle XKY \).

<span style="color:#00f">**<u>Lemma 2</u>**</span>: Let \( ABCD \) be a convex quadrilateral with \( (M, N) \) an isogonal conjugate pair with respect to quadrilateral \( ABCD \). Then there exists an ellipse \( \mathcal{E} \) with foci \( M, N \) which is tangent to every side of \( ABCD \).


