*<span style="color:#351C75">Proof of Claim</span>*: Let  $P$  be the miquel point of  $D,E,F$ . Now,  $(AEFP)$  has diameter  $AE$ , so  $DE \perp AE$  implies  $DE$  is tangent to  $AEFP$ . Similarly,  $EF$  is tangent to  $(BFDP)$  and  $FD$  is tangent to  $(CDEP)$ . Thus,  $\angle PCB = \angle PCD = \angle PDF = \angle PBF = \angle PBA$ . Similarly  $\angle PBA = \angle PAC$ . Thus  $P$  is the Brocard point, and  $D,E,F$  are the unique points on  $BC,CA,AB$  respectively such that  $\angle CPD = \angle APE = \angle BPF = 90^\circ$   $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:#351C75">Claim 2:</span>** The circumcenter of  $\triangle DEF$  is  $K$ , and the radius is  $R \tan \omega$ , where  $\omega$  is the Brocard angle of  $\triangle ABC$ .
*<span style="color:#351C75">Proof of Claim 2</span>*: Consider the lines through  $K$  antiparallel to the sides of the triangle, it is well known the points of intersection of these with  $BC,CA,AB$  have the requisite property and hence by uniqueness these are our given  $D,E,F$ . The claim about the radius follows from noting that  $\triangle ABC \sim \triangle EFD$ , both have Brocard point  $P$  so the spiral center that sends one to the other must be  $P$  with angle  $90^\circ$  and ratio being  $PD/PC$  which is  $\tan \omega$ .  $\blacksquare$ So, we are reduced to showing that  $OK = R \tan \omega$  if and only if  $OK + R \tan \omega = R$ .**<span style="color:#351C75">Claim 3:</span>**  $OK^2 = R \cdot \dfrac{1-4\sin^2 \omega}{\cos^2 \omega}.$ *<span style="color:#351C75">Proof of Claim 3</span>*: As noted above, Spiral similarity at  $P$  with angle  $90^\circ$  and ratio  $\tan \omega$  sends  $\triangle ABC$  to  $\triangle EFD$ . Therefore it sends  $O$  to  $K$ , and hence  $\angle OPK = 90^\circ$  and  $PK/PO = \tan \omega$ . Thus,  $OK^2 = PO^2 + PK^2 = PO^2(1+\tan^2 \omega) = PO^2 \sec^2 \omega$ . So it remains to show that  $R^2 - PO^2 = 4R^2 \sin^2 \omega$ . This follows from noting that   $$ AP = \frac{2R \sin C \cdot \sin \omega}{\sin A}, PT = \frac{2R \sin \omega \sin A}{ \sin C}  $$   where  $T$  is the second intersection of  $AP$  with  $(ABC)$  and then applying power of a point.  $\blacksquare$ Now, a direct calculation finishes: (note that  $\omega$  is always acute)  $OK = R \tan \omega \iff \sin^2 \omega = 1/5$ . Similarly,  $OK = R (1 - \tan \omega) \iff 2 \sin \omega = \cos \omega \iff \sin^2 \omega = 1/5$ .  $\spadesuit$

