Answer:  $\boxed{c = 4.}$ This is achieved by
\[f(n)=
\begin{cases}
1 & \quad\text{if  $n$  is odd,}
16 & \quad\text{if  $n=4$ ,}
2 & \quad\text{else.}
\end{cases}
\]
Let  $P(a, b)$  denote the given assertion and let  $f$  satisfy  $P$ .  $P(a, a)$  yields  $f(a) \mid a^a$ , so  $f(1) = 1$ , and for prime  $p$ ,  $f(p) \in \{1, p, p^2, \dots, p^p\}$ .**Claim I:** If  $f(p) > 1$  for any prime  $p$ , then  $b \equiv f(b) \pmod{p}$  for all  $b$ .
*Proof:* Let  $f(p) = p^h$ ,  $h > 0$ .  $P(p, b) \Rightarrow p^h \mid b^p - f(b)^{p^h}$  but we have the below by FLT.   $$ b^p - f(b)^{p^h} = b^p - \underbrace{( \dots (f(b))^p)^p \dots )^p}_{h \text{times}} \equiv b - f(b) \equiv 0 \pmod{p} $$  We're going to approach via casework on  $f(3)$ .**Case 1:**  $f(3) > 1$ .
By claim I,  $b \equiv f(b) \pmod{3}$  for all  $b$ . One can assert that there are infinitely many primes congruent to  $2$  mod  $3$  via Dirichlet's or by <details><summary>elementary proof.</summary>If there were only finitely many primes  $p_1, \dots, p_m$  congruent to  $2$  mod  $3$ , consider  $N = 3p_1 \cdots p_m - 1$  which no  $p_i$  divides and which is  $2$  mod  $3$ . If  $N$  is prime, it's a prime other than all  $p_i$  that's  $2$  mod  $3$ . If  $N$  factorizes only into primes  $1$  mod  $3$ ,  $N \equiv 1 \mod 3$ . Hence  $N$  is divisible by a prime  $p_{m+1} \equiv 2 \mod 3$  distinct from  $p_i$ , contradicting finiteness.</details> 

Thus for all such primes  $q \equiv 2 \pmod 3$  we have  $f(q) > 1$ . By Claim I,  $b \equiv f(b) \pmod{q}$  for all  $b$ , so take  $q > b^b$  large so that  $|b - f(b)| < q \Rightarrow b = f(b)$  for all  $b$ . Here  $f(n)/n$  is  $1$  at best.**Case 2:**  $f(3) = 1$ .
For  $p$  an odd prime,  $P(p, 3) \Rightarrow p^h \mid 3^p - 1$  with  $3^p - 1 \equiv 2 \pmod{p}$  by FLT. Hence  $f(p) = 1$ .

Then, let  $n$  be a composite and say its prime factorization consists of odd primes  $a_1, \dots, a_t$ . Suppose  $a_i \mid f(n)$ . Then as  $a_i \nmid a_i^n - 1$ ,  $P(n, a_i)$  is false. Hence  $f(n)$  must be a power of  $2$  for  $n$  composite (which means  $f(n) = 1$  for all odd  $n$ ). 

Let  $j$  be even. For all odd  $k$ ,  $P(j, k) \Rightarrow f(j) \mid k^j - 1$ . We impose an upper bound on  $\nu_2(f(j))$  by considering  $k = 3$ , so that by LTE  $\nu_2(f(j)) \le \nu_2(3^j - 1) = \nu_2(4) + \nu_2(2) + \nu_2(j) - 1 = \nu_2(j) + 2$ . Hence,  $f(j) \le 4j$ .

P.S. No one saw my deleted fakesolve where I asserted that  $q^n$  takes distinct values mod  $p$  for all odd primes  $p$ ,  $q$ .

