A Problem Based on ANGLE CHASING. $\color{red}\textbf{Claim:-}$  Let  $ME\cap NF=T$  and  $ME\cap (BEF)=X \implies $   $X$  and  $T$  are coincides. $\color{blue}\textbf{Proof:-}$  Let  $AB\cap CD=Q.$  Extend  $PM$  and  $PN$  to meet the sides  $AC$  and  $AD$  at points  $Y$  and  $Z$  By Angle Chasing we get, let  $\angle MAC = \theta$  and  $\angle NAD = \alpha$    $$ \angle MAC=\angle MCA=180-2 \angle AMC=2\angle AMN=2\angle BMQ=90-2 \angle MAQ=90-2 \angle MBQ=\angle MCB=\angle MBC=2\angle  $$     $$ \implies\angle APD=180-2 \angle MEB=2\angle BEF. $$   Also We get,   $$ \angle NAD=\angle NDA=180-2\angle AND=2\angle ANM=2\angle APC=2\angle EMN $$   Therefore the points  $N,D,P,F$  and  $M,E,P,C$  are concyclic. Since  $\angle QNF=\angle FPD$  and  $\angle AEM=\angle MCP$  
Also By Angle Chasing we get that the quadrilateral  $AMTN$  is a parallelogram  $\implies \boxed{\angle BAE=\theta-\alpha.}$  Again By Angle Chasing we get that   $$ \angle CPD=2(\theta+\alpha)\implies \angle MAE=\angle MEA=\angle FEX=90-(\theta+\alpha) $$   After Lots of Angle Chasing we conclude that  $\angle TFE=90-(\theta+\alpha)$  Now Join  $FN$  Finally in cyclic quadrilateral  $FNDP$  we get,   $$ \angle NFP=90+(\theta+\alpha) $$   Since  $A,F,P$  are collinear thus we get,   $$ \angle TFB=\angle NXA=90-(\theta+\alpha) $$   Therefore it implies that  $X$  and  $T$  coincides with each other  $\implies$   $\boxed{T\in (BEF).}$  $\color{red}\textbf{Claim:-}$   $H$  is the incentre of  $\triangle TMN$  and  $HT\parallel AP.$  $\color{blue}\textbf{Proof:-}$  By Angle Chasing we get,  $\angle APN=\theta$  and  $\angle APM=\alpha$  therefore  $\angle PCD=90-(\theta+\alpha)$  
Now Since  $AMTN$  is a parallelogram, therefore we have  $\angle MNT= \angle AMN =2\theta$  and  $\angle NMT=\angle MNA=2\alpha.$  Now By Angle Chasing we get,   $$ \angle MNH=90-\angle PMN=90-(\angle PCD+\alpha)=\theta $$   Finally By Angle Chasing we get,   $$ \angle MNH=\angle TNH=\theta \implies \angle NMH=\angle TMH=\alpha $$   Therefore  $H$  is the incentre of  $\triangle TMN.$  Hence  $\angle MTH=\angle NTH=90-(\theta+\alpha)$  Therefore Last Angle Chase gives us   $$ \angle THE=\angle TFE=\angle TEF\implies \boxed{HT \parallel AP.} $$

Let alice be amin and bazza be ali If  $\lambda < \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$ , Ali has the winning strategy. If  $\lambda > \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$ , Amin has it. If  $\lambda = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$ , there is no winning strategy, both have a non-losing strategy, and the game is a draw.

In each step, Ali sets  $X_{2k}$  equal to  $\sqrt{2 - X_{2k-1}^2}$ . We will prove why he can do this, and after that, we will prove that Ali wins.

Assume he has proceeded with this strategy until step  $k-1$  and now cannot implement this strategy. This means we have:
\begin{align*}
2 - X_{2k-1}^{2} &< 0 
X_{1} + \dots + X_{2k-1} &\le \lambda(2k-1) 
\sqrt{2} &< X_{2k-1}
\end{align*}

On the other hand, we have:
\[ X_{2k-1} \le \lambda(2k-1) - (X_1 + X_2) - (X_3 + X_4) - \dots - (X_{2k-3} + X_{2k-2}) \]

Also, according to this strategy, we have:
\begin{align*}
X_{2i}^{2} &= \sqrt{2 - X_{2i-1}^{2}} \Rightarrow X_{2i}^{2} + X_{2i-1}^{2} = 2 
X_{1}^{2} + X_{2}^{2} &= \dots = X_{2k-2}^{2} + X_{2k-1}^{2}
\end{align*}

\[ X_{2k-1} \le \lambda(2k-1) - \sqrt{2}(k-1) \]

Assuming  $\lambda \le \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$ :
\begin{align*}
X_{2k-1} &\le \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}(2k-1) - \sqrt{2}k + \sqrt{2} 
&= \sqrt{2}k - \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} - \sqrt{2}k + \sqrt{2} 
&= \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}
\end{align*}

\[ X_{2k-1} \le \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \]
\[ X_{1} \le \lambda \le \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \Rightarrow X_{1}^{2} < \frac{2}{4} \]
\[ 2 - X_{1}^2 > 0 \text{ so Contradiction} \]

Consider the case where  $k=1$ . So, we have that Ali can always use this strategy.

Now, for any  $\alpha$  we have:
\begin{align*}
X_{1} + X_{2} + \dots + X_{2\alpha} + X_{2\alpha+1} &\le \lambda(2\alpha + 1) 
X_{1} + X_{2} + \dots + X_{2\alpha} &\ge \sqrt{2}\alpha \text{ and } X_{2\alpha+1} \ge 0 
\Rightarrow \sqrt{2}\alpha &\le \lambda(2\alpha + 1) 
\Rightarrow \frac{\sqrt{2}\alpha}{2\alpha + 1} &\le \lambda
\end{align*}

Now if we make  $\alpha$  large, since  $\frac{\alpha}{2\alpha + 1}$  approaches  $\frac{1}{2}$ , then:
\[ \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \le \lambda \]

For large values of  $\alpha$  we have:
\[ \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \le \lambda < \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \]
Therefore, based on this, Amin has not been always able to introduce another  $X$ .

For every  $u$ , Amin sets his  $X_{2u-1}$  to zero. Now if ali not lose, for all  $k$  we have:
\[ \sum_{j=1}^{k} X_{2j}^2 \] $\le$ 2k

Now according to Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have:
\begin{align*}
(X_2^2 + X_4^2 + \dots + X_{2k}^2)(1 + 1 + \dots + 1) &\ge (X_2 + X_4 + \dots + X_{2k})^2 
k(X_2^2 + X_4^2 + \dots + X_{2k}^2) &\ge (X_2 + X_4 + \dots + X_{2k})^2 
2k^2 &\ge (X_1 + \dots + X_{2k})^2 
\Rightarrow X_2 + \dots + X_{2k} &\ge \sqrt{2}k
\end{align*}

Now we have:
\begin{align*}
X_2 + \dots + X_{2k} + X_{2k+1} &\le \lambda(2k + 1) 
T=\lambda(2k + 1) - X_1 - \dots - X_{2k}  and
 T &\ge \sqrt{2}k + \lambda(-2k + 1), \text{ with } \lambda \ge \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} 
T &\ge \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2} \Rightarrow T \ge 0
\end{align*}

Now according to this, he can set  $X_{2k+1}$ =T. Now if ali not lose , there exists a  $k$  such that:
\[ X_1^2 + \dots + X_{2k}^2 + X_{2k+1}^2 + X_{2k+2}^2 \le 2k + 2 \]

\begin{align*}
X_{2k+1} &= \lambda(2k + 1) - X_1 - \dots - X_{2k} 
X_1^2 + \dots + X_{2k}^2 &+ (\lambda(2k + 1) - X_1 - \dots - X_{2k})^2 + X_{2k+2}^2 \le 2k + 2
\end{align*}

According to Cauchy-Schwarz:
\begin{align*}
(X_1^2 + \dots + X_{2k}^2 &+ (\lambda(2k + 1) - \dots)^2)\left(\frac{1 + \dots + 1}{k + 1}\right) \ge (\lambda(2k + 1))^2 
\Rightarrow X_1^2 + \dots + X_{2k}^2 &+ (\lambda(2k + 1) - \dots)^2 \ge \frac{\lambda^2(2k + 1)^2}{k + 1} 
X_{2k+2}^2 + \frac{\lambda^2(2k + 1)^2}{k + 1} &\le 2k + 2 
X_{2k+2}^2 &\ge 0 \Rightarrow \frac{\lambda^2(2k + 1)^2}{k + 1} \le 2k + 2 
\lambda^2 &\le \frac{2(k + 1)^2}{(2k + 1)^2} = A
\end{align*}

Now if  $k$  is very large,  $A$  approaches  $\frac{1}{2}$ , and on the other hand, since  $\lambda > \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$ , this is a contradiction.

So Amin can play long enough (by setting his moves to zero) until the indices of  $X$  become so large that Ali cannot play.

Now why can Amin always play zero?
Assume the last zero he wrote was  $X_{2k}$ . Now if he has won, it means Ali wanted to introduce  $X_{2k+1}$  and according to the relation we have:
\[ X_1 + X_3 + \dots + X_{2k-1} \le \sqrt{2}k \]

Now it is sufficient to prove that Amin can set the  $(k + 1)$ -th move,  $X_{2(k + 1)}$ , to zero.
\begin{align*}
\lambda(2k + 1) - \sqrt{2}k &\ge 0 
\Rightarrow (2k + 1)\lambda &\ge \sqrt{2}k 
\lambda &\ge \frac{\sqrt{2}k}{2k + 1}
\end{align*}
So Amin can always play zero. 

Let  $\lambda = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$ . Now notice that in the reasoning, the assumption  $\lambda > \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$  or  $\lambda < \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$  was only used at the very end of the problem, and the rest of the lemmas and claims were with the assumption  $\lambda = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$ . 

According to this, Ali can always play  $X_{2k} = \sqrt{2 - X_{2k-1}^2}$ , so he never loses, and, Amin can set his  $X_{2k+1}$   to zero, so he never loses either. 

Thus, this situation continues to infinity if both play their best game, and we will not have a winner.

The problem concludes as follows:

    For  $\lambda > \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$ , Amin wins the game
    For  $\lambda < \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$ , Ali wins the game
    For  $\lambda = \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$ , the game will have no winner

