**Solution** By Power Mean Inequality,  $\forall k>1$ , and  $\forall x\in\mathbb{R}^+$ ,  $$ \left(\frac{1+x^k}{2}\right)^\frac{1}{k}\ge\frac{1+x}{2}\implies\frac {1+x^k}{1+x}\ge\frac{(1+x)^{k-1}}{2^{k-1}}, $$  which, together with AM-GM Inequality as well as Hölder’s Inequality, implies that 
\begin{align*} & \frac{1 + x_1^k}{1 + x_2} + \frac{1 + x_2^k}{1 + x_3} + \cdots + \frac{1 + x_n^k}{1 + x_1}  
\ge&n\sqrt[n]{ \frac{\left(1 + x_1^k\right) \left(1 + x_2^k\right)\cdots \left(1 + x_n^k\right)}{(1 + x_1)(1 + x_2) \cdots (1 + x_n)}} 
\ge&n\sqrt[n]{ \frac{\left[\left(1 + x_1\right)\left(1 + x_2\right)\cdots \left(1 + x_n\right)\right]^{k-1}}{2^{n(k-1)}}} \ge&n\sqrt[n]{ \frac{\left[\left(1 + \sqrt[n]{x_1x_2\cdots  x_n}\right)^n\right]^{k-1}}{2^{n(k-1)}}}=n, 
\end{align*}and the minimum value  $n$  can be attained if  $x_i=1\,\,\left(\forall i\in\{1,\cdots,n\}\right)$ .  $\blacksquare$

