For  $N$ , we ignore the other  $N-T_k$  red cells. We now show that  $k$  is maximal; suppose we have  $\ell$  ruby rooks. For each of the  $\binom{\ell}{2}$  pairs of ruby rooks, there exists an empty red cell they are both connected to. Now if we produce the same empty red cell for two distinct pairs of ruby rooks, then at least  $3$  ruby rooks are connected to that empty cell, and so some pair of them lie on the same row or column, which is impossible. So we produce distinct empty red cells from each pair, and there are at least  $\binom{\ell}{2}+\ell$  red cells in total. From  $\binom{\ell}{2}+\ell<T_{k+1}$  we get  $\ell\leq k$ , as desired. We can now compute  $k=63$ .

