Consider a snake as a sequence of cells  $c_1, c_2, \dots, c_{n^2}$  in some starting direction. To get the desired bound, it is suffice to show that at least  $n-1$  cells that is not the  $2$  ends do not contain a turn.
[rule]**<span style="color:#f00">Claim</span>**: For any snake, at least  $n-1$  uncolored square does not contain a  $90^{\circ}$  turn.**<span style="color:#00f">Proof</span>** : Consider a subsequence of a snake consisting all colored cells. One may notice that the sequence is of the form  $c_{\text{odd}}$  or  $c_{\text{even}}$ . Notice that an uncolored cell  $c_i$  has a turn if  $2$  cells  $c_{i+1}$  and  $c_{i-1}$  are of different colors. 
But number of green cells are less than that of purple cells.So, the number of uncolored cells with a turn is at most twice the number of green cells( That is the maximum number of pairs of colored cells  $(c_{i}, c_{i+1})$ , such that at least one is green). Hence, we have at most   $$ \frac{n^2-1}{2} - 2(\frac{n-1}{2})^2 = \frac{n^2-1 - n^2 +2n -1}{2} = n-1 $$   as desired.  $\blacksquare$ [rule]
