We claim the only solution is  $f(x)=0$ , which obviously works. We now show that there are no other solutions. Consider a solution  $f$ .

Define  $g,h\colon S\longrightarrow\mathbb Z$  with
\begin{align*}
    f(x)&=g(x)-h(x)
    f(x^2)&=2g(x)-h(x)
\end{align*}
for all  $x\in S$ . Now for any  $x,y\in S$ , \[f(x^2)+f(y)=f(x^2y+c)=f(x)+f(xy)\implies f(xy)=g(x)+g(y)-h(y)\] Performing a similar argument with  $x,y$  swapped, we get  $h(x)=h(y)$ . Then  $h$  is some constant  $d$ , and  $g(x)=f(x)+d$ . We also get \[g(xy)=g(x)+g(y)\]

The given condition becomes \[g(z+c)=g(z)+d\] for all  $z$  of the form  $xy$  for  $x,y\in S$ . Let  $A$  be the set of  $z$  expressable in this form, and let  $B$  be the set of  $z$  with  $g(z+c)=g(z)$ . For any  $z\in A$ , we have  $z(z+c+1)\in A$  and
\begin{align*}
    &g(z+c+1)
    =&g(z(z+c+1))-g(z)
    =&g((z+1)(z+c))-g(z)-d
    =&g(z+1)+g(z+c)-g(z)-d
    =&g(z+1)
\end{align*}
Then taking  $z\in A$  with  $z+1\in A$ , for example by taking  $z$  with
\begin{align*}
    z&\equiv 0\pmod{p^2}
    z&\equiv -1\pmod{q^2}
\end{align*}
for some relatively prime  $p,q\in S$ , yields that  $d=0$ . Then \[z\in A\implies z\in B\] Now suppose we have  $z\in A\implies z+k\in B$  for some  $k\geq 0$ . Then for any  $z\in A$ , we have  $(z+k)(z+k+c+1)\in A$  and
\begin{align*}
    &g(z+k+c+1)
    =&g((z+k)(z+k+c+1))-g(z+k)
    =&g((z+k+1)(z+k+c))-g(z+k)
    =&g(z+k+1)+g(z+k+c)-g(z+k)
    =&g(z+k+1)
\end{align*}
and  $z+k+1\in B$ . Since we already have this for  $k=0$ , we now have \[z\in A\implies z+k\in B\] for all  $k$  by induction. It follows that  $g$  is eventually periodic with period  $c$ . But then for all  $x\in S$ , taking sufficiently large  $m>n$  with  $c\mid x^m-x^n$ , we get \[g(x^m)=g(x^n)\implies g(x)=0\] Then  $f(x)=0$  for all  $x$ , as desired.

