A bit more general framework; let  $s_k=4 \cdot 5^{k-3}+1$ , where  $k \geqslant 3$ . By induction on  $k$  we prove that from  $s_k$  real numbers it is possible to choose a good sequence of length  $k$ . he base  $k=3$  is not complicated. Indeed, let the numbers  $b_1 \leqslant b_2 \leqslant b_3 \leqslant b_4 \leqslant b_5$  be given. Among the differences  $|b_k-b_{k+1}|$  we choose the smallest one, let it be the difference  $|b_i-b_{i+1}|$ . If  $i \leqslant 2$ , then the sequence  $a_1=b_i, a_2=b_{i+1}$  and  $a_3=b_{i+3}$  is good. If  $i \geqslant 3$ , then the sequence  $a_1=b_{i+1}, a_2=b_i$  and  $a_3=b_{i-2}$  is good.

For the transition  $k \to k+1$ . Note that  $s_{k+1}=5 s_k-4$ . Let's arrange the numbers in ascending order and divide them sequentially into 5 groups

so that adjacent groups have a common extreme number:  $$ \begin{aligned}
b_1 & \leqslant \ldots \leqslant b_{s_k} 
b_{s_k} & \leqslant \ldots \leqslant b_{2 s_k-1} 
b_{2 s_k-1} & \leqslant \ldots \leqslant b_{3 s_k-2} 
b_{3 s_k-2} & \leqslant \ldots \leqslant b_{4 s_k-3} 
b_{4 s_k-3} & \leqslant \ldots \leqslant b_{5 s_k-4}=b_{s_{k+1}}
\end{aligned} $$  For each group, we calculate the difference between the maximum and minimum numbers. We denote the smallest of these differences by  $m$ , let it be realized in the  $r$ -th group. By the induction hypothesis, we choose a good sequence  $a_1, \ldots, a_k$  in the  $r$ -th group. Obviously, the inequality  $|a_k-a_1| \leqslant m$  holds for it. We need to add one more number to the constructed sequence.

If  $p \leqslant 2$ , then we set  $a_{k+1}=b_{s_{k+1}}$ , then  $$ |a_{k+1}-a_1| \geqslant 3 m \geqslant 3|a_k-a_1| $$  and the required property is satisfied. Similarly, for  $r \geqslant 4$  we set  $a_{k+1}=b_1$ . Finally, let  $r=3$ . If  $a_1<a_k$ , then we set  $a_{k+1}=b_{s_{k+1}}$  and then  $$ |a_{k+1}-a_1| \geqslant|a_k-a_1|+2 m \geqslant 3|a_k-a_1| . $$  Similarly, for  $a_1>a_k$  we set  $a_{k+1}=b_1$ .
It only remains to note that  $314159>312501=s_{10}$ .

