It is easy to see that  $(AFE),(BDF)$ , and  $(CDE)$  pass through the incenter of  $\triangle{ABC}$ .
We now have  $\angle{FPQ}=\angle{EPR}$  (due to parallel lines of  $PQ,AB$  and  $PR,AC$ ).
Also, the same result yields for another angles (as shown in the figure).

Let  $A=\angle{BAC},B=\angle{ABC},C=\angle{ACB},\angle{FPQ}=\alpha,\angle{RQD}=\beta$  and  $\angle{ERP}=\gamma$ .
Also, denote  $E_1=EQ\cap PR, F_1=FR\cap PQ$  and  $D_1=PD\cap QR$ . We have
\begin{align*}
    \frac{QD_1}{RD_1}=\frac{[PQD]}{[PRD]}=\frac{PQ\cdot QD\sin(B+\beta)}{PR\cdot RD\sin(C+\gamma)}
    \frac{RE_1}{PE_1}=\frac{[REQ]}{[PEQ]}=\frac{RE\cdot RQ\sin(C+\gamma)}{PE\cdot PQ\sin(A+\alpha)}
    \frac{PF_1}{QF_1}=\frac{[PFR]}{[QFR]}=\frac{PF\cdot PR\sin(A+\alpha)}{FQ\cdot RQ\sin(B+\beta)}
\end{align*}
Multiply these equations:
\begin{align*}
    \frac{QD_1}{RD_1}\cdot \frac{RE_1}{PE_1}\cdot \frac{PF_1}{QF_1}&=\frac{QD}{RD}\cdot \frac{RE}{PE}\cdot \frac{PF}{PQ}
    &=\frac{\sin(\gamma)}{\sin(\beta)}\cdot \frac{\sin(\alpha)}{\sin(\gamma)}\cdot \frac{\sin(\beta)}{\sin(\alpha)}
    &=1.
\end{align*}
Which means that  $PD, EQ$  and  $FR$  are concurrent as desired.

