Official Solution by the proposer Ahmet İleri

The answer is  $\boxed{\varphi(n)}$ . All  $k$  positive integers that are relatively prime with  $n$  satisfies the condition if we take  $a_i=0$ .**Lemma.** Let  $p$  be a prime and  $n$  be a positive integer such that  $p|n$ . Then   $$ v_p(1^{p-1}+2^{p-1}+\cdots+n^{p-1})=v_p(n)-1 $$  *Proof.* Denote  $S(n)=1^{p-1}+2^{p-1}+\cdots+n^{p-1}$ . First, we will show that the claim holds for  $n=p^k$ , and then by using that, for  $n=p^km$ . For  $n=p^k$ , we will use induction. In the base case,  $n=p$ , we have  $S(p)\equiv p-1\pmod{p}$ . Now assume the claim holds for  $n=p^k$  for some  $k\geq 1$ . Then, for a positive integer  $c$ , since  $2k\geq k+1$ , we have   $$ \sum_{a=1}^{p^k}(cp^k+a)^{p-1}\equiv \sum_{a=1}^{p^k}(a^{p-1}+a^{p-2}cp^k(p-1))\pmod{p^{k+1}} $$  Summing up these values for all  $0\leq c\leq p-1$ , we get 
    \begin{align*}
        S(p^{k+1}) &\equiv \sum_{c=0}^{p-1}\left(\sum_{a=1}^{p^k}(a^{p-1}+a^{p-2}cp^k(p-1))\right)\pmod{p^{k+1}}
                   &\equiv p S(p)+\left(\sum_{a=1}^{p^k}a^{p-2}\right)\left(\sum_{c=0}^{p-1} c\right)p^k(p-1)\pmod{p^{k+1}}
                   &\equiv pS(p)\pmod{p^{k+1}}
    \end{align*}
    From that, we have  $v_p(S(p^{k+1}))=v_p(S(p^k))+1=(k-1)+1=k$ . Now, in the other case where  $n=p^km$ , we get  $S(n)\equiv mS(p^k)\pmod{p^k}$  and since  $v_p(S(p^k))=k-1$ , we get  $v_p(S(p^km))=v_p(S(p^k))=k-1=v_p(n)-1$ .  $\square$ Now, the crucial claim is that for any  $p|n$ , there exists a  $f(p)$  such that if  $k\equiv f(p)\pmod{p}$ , then  $a_i+ki$  can not satisfy the property. This claim finishes the problem with the help of CRT.

For the proof of this claim, take  $p|n$  and assume that there exists  $1\leq x_1,x_2,\cdots,x_p\leq n$  such that  $x_i\equiv i\pmod{p}$  and the sequences  $a_i+ki$  for  $k=x_i$  all satisfy the property. Define  $F(k)=\sum_{i=1}^n(a_i+ki)^{p-1}$ . By the lemma, we have  $v_p(F(k))=v_p(n)-1$  for  $k=x_i$ , since  $a_i+ki$  produces a complete residue class in  $\pmod{n}$ . Let  $d=v_p(n)-1$ . Then, we have  $F(x_1)\equiv F(x_2)\equiv\cdots\equiv F(x_p)\equiv p^dt\pmod{n}$  for a positive integer  $t$  with  $(t,p)=1$ . So,  $p^{d+1}\vert\sum_{i=1}^nF(x_i)$ On the other hand, we have   $$ F(k)=\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^{p-1}+{p-1\choose 1}k^1\left(\sum_{i=1}^na_i^{p-2}i^1\right)+\cdots+k^{p-1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^n i^{p-1}\right) $$  Let  $P(y)=A_0+A_1y+\cdots+A_{p-1}y^{p-1}$  be a polynomial in  $\mathbb{Z}[x]$  where  $A_j={p-1\choose j}\left(\sum_{i=1}^na_i^{p-1-j}i^j\right)$ . We have  $p^d|P(x_i)$  for all  $x_i$ , and since  $x_i$  values are different from each other in  $\pmod{p}$  and this polynomial has degree at most  $p-1$ , it must be the zero polynomial. That is,  $p|A_j$  for all  $j$ . The same argument holds for  $Q(y)=\frac{P(y)}{p}$  since  $p^{d-1}|Q(x_i)$ , and we finally obtain  $p^d|A_j$  for all  $j$ . 

Now, consider  $F(x_1)+F(x_2)+\cdots+F(x_p)$ . Let  $s_j=x_1^j+x_2^j+\cdots+x_p^j$ . Then,  $F(x_1)+F(x_2)+\cdots+F(x_p)=s_0A_0+s_1A_1+\cdots+s_{p-1}A_{p-1}$ . It is well known that  $p|s_j$  for  $0\leq j\leq p-2$  and  $p\nmid s_{p-1}$ . Thus,  $p^{d+1}|s_jA_j$  for  $0\leq j\leq p-2$  and  $p^{d+1}\nmid s_{p-1}A_{p-1}$ , so  $p^{d+1}\nmid F(x_1)+F(x_2)+\cdots+F(x_p)$ . That is a contradiction.

We claim we can place at most  $12$  knights. The construction is shown below where each shaded square represents a square with a knight:
[asy]
size(150);
