}
[/asy]
We show we cannot place more than  $12$ . **Claim**: We cannot place  $4$  or more knights in a  $4 \times 4$  grid.
*Proof*: Divide the squares of a  $4 \times 4$  grid as follows:
[asy]
size(70);
int n = 4;

// Draw grid
for (int i = 0; i <= n; ++i) {
  draw((i,0)--(i,n), black+1.2bp); // vertical lines
  draw((0,i)--(n,i), black+1.2bp); // horizontal lines
}

// Light gray corners
pair[] lightGray = {
  (0,0), (0,3), (3,0), (3,3)
};

// Dark gray center 2x2 block
pair[] darkGray = {
  (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (2,2)
};

// Fill light gray cells
for (pair p : lightGray) {
  fill((p.x,p.y)--(p.x+1,p.y)--(p.x+1,p.y+1)--(p.x,p.y+1)--cycle, gray);
}

// Fill dark gray cells
for (pair p : darkGray) {
  fill((p.x,p.y)--(p.x+1,p.y)--(p.x+1,p.y+1)--(p.x,p.y+1)--cycle, black);
}
[/asy]
If there are  $2$  knights on gray squares, them being on the same diagonal will guarantee a black square that can be reached by both of them in one move, and them being on the same side will guarantee that the remaining squares in the  $4 \times 4$  grid will be reachable by one of those knights in at most two moves.

If there are  $2$  knights on black squares, them being on the same diagonal will guarantee a gray square that can be reached by both of them in one move, and them being on the same side will guarantee that the remaining squares in the  $4 \times 4$  grid will be reachable by one of those knights in at most two moves.

Hence, there must be at least  $2$  knights on white squares. Label the white squares as follows:
[asy]
