We see that we cannot place two knights on squares labeled A or two knights on squares labeled B. Hence, we can only have exactly  $2$  knights on white squares. If we want to place at least  $4$  knights on this  $4 \times 4$  grid, the only way is to have knights on  $2$  white,  $1$  black, and  $1$  gray squares. 

WLOG we place a knight on the bottom left gray square. Then the only black squares we can place a knight on are the two on the bottom left-top right diagonal. In either case, only two remaining squares will be not reachable in at most two moves from either knight. But placing knights on both of them will result in violating the condition.

Therefore, the claim is proven. Each  $4 \times 4$  grid can have at most  $3$  knights, so we can place at most  $12$  knights in total.  $\Box$

