Indeed, by the condition that no number occurs more than  $\frac{n^2+n}{2}$  times, no two rows of the table will be identical. Suppose otherwise, that the  $k^{\text{th}}$  row is equal to a row below it. Then it is also equal to the  $(k+1)^{\text{th}}$  row.

Clearly all the numbers of the table lying between the antidiagonals containing the leftmost end of the  $k^{\text{th}}$  row and the rightmost end of the  $(k+1)^{\text{th}}$  row are equal (blue cells in the figure). The number of the remaining (red) cells is equal to  $\frac{k^2+k}{2}-1+\frac{(n-k)^2+(n-k)}{2}-1< \frac{n^2+n}{2}$  [tip=*]this is a quadratic inequality in  $k$  whence it suffices to check only  $k=1$  and  $k=n-1$ [/tip] which is contradiction with the condition.

Thus we can group the numbers by the rows of the table.

